1
00:01:42,402 --> 00:01:44,303
No! I'm not gonna go!

2
00:01:44,471 --> 00:01:47,464
I hate seafood! It's gross!

3
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:49,701
I wanna have hamburgers.

4
00:01:49,876 --> 00:01:51,640
Jason Michael Terwilliger,

5
00:01:51,812 --> 00:01:54,338
you have five seconds.

6
00:01:56,216 --> 00:02:00,176
One, two...

7
00:02:20,874 --> 00:02:24,834
Yes. My name is Ellen Barrett.

8
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:29,382
I'm calling from Room
227 of the Sea View Motel.

9
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:33,019
I think I killed someone.

10
00:02:34,921 --> 00:02:37,481
Uh-huh. Yes, I'll be here.

11
00:02:47,334 --> 00:02:48,334
Ellen Barrett.

12
00:02:48,502 --> 00:02:49,834
That's two R's and two T's?

13
00:02:50,003 --> 00:02:52,336
Yes. I'm from Atlanta.

14
00:02:52,506 --> 00:02:55,101
I'm a journalist. I write
a newspaper column.

15
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:56,504
And he is?

16
00:02:57,744 --> 00:02:59,406
Was?

17
00:02:59,746 --> 00:03:02,580
He said his name
was Adam Easley.

18
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:04,611
He worked for Vic Baron.

19
00:03:05,786 --> 00:03:08,449
Vic Baron as in the mobster?

20
00:03:08,889 --> 00:03:10,755
Easley called me
about a week ago

21
00:03:10,924 --> 00:03:14,622
and said that he heard that I
was doing a story about his boss.

22
00:03:14,795 --> 00:03:16,957
And that he and Baron
had had a falling out,

23
00:03:17,130 --> 00:03:20,430
and he'd be more than
happy to rat on his boss.

24
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:24,303
I thought he was on the
level so I agreed to meet him.

25
00:03:24,471 --> 00:03:26,702
And then when I got here...

26
00:03:26,907 --> 00:03:29,001
What happened, Miss Barrett?

27
00:03:30,877 --> 00:03:33,904
Well, it was a setup.

28
00:03:34,281 --> 00:03:35,579
I'd no sooner shut the door

29
00:03:35,749 --> 00:03:38,344
but he grabbed me and he
told me that Baron wanted me

30
00:03:38,518 --> 00:03:40,544
to drop the story or else.

31
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:45,125
And then he started to throw
me around the room and he hit me,

32
00:03:45,292 --> 00:03:47,318
until finally I...

33
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:50,264
Maybe we should do
this someplace else.

34
00:04:07,447 --> 00:04:10,417
- Feeling better?
- Yeah.

35
00:04:10,717 --> 00:04:14,449
- Okay. Do you mind if I turn this on?
- Go ahead.

36
00:04:16,623 --> 00:04:20,617
Now, in as much detail
as you can remember,

37
00:04:20,794 --> 00:04:24,561
tell me exactly what happened
in that motel room tonight.

38
00:04:28,668 --> 00:04:33,129
Well, after I told Easley that I
wasn't about to drop the story,

39
00:04:33,306 --> 00:04:38,267
and that he and Vic Baron
could pretty much shove it,

40
00:04:38,712 --> 00:04:43,013
he grabbed me by the hair and
he threw me across the room.

41
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:47,610
I hit a chair on the way
down, and I knocked it over.

42
00:04:47,788 --> 00:04:49,222
And then I got up pretty fast,

43
00:04:49,389 --> 00:04:53,588
and I grabbed for the phone
to try to call for some help,

44
00:04:55,128 --> 00:04:59,793
and he yanked the receiver out
of my hand and he hit me with it.

45
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:01,559
Hard.

46
00:05:02,435 --> 00:05:03,733
Then what happened?

47
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:06,704
Well, then I scratched his face.

48
00:05:06,873 --> 00:05:09,809
And I tried to run for the
door, but he pulled me back,

49
00:05:09,976 --> 00:05:14,175
and he grabbed my blouse
and he started to slap me.

50
00:05:14,347 --> 00:05:17,613
I thought he was gonna kill me,

51
00:05:19,052 --> 00:05:21,419
so that's when I
saw the knife, and...

52
00:05:21,588 --> 00:05:26,322
It was sitting on a tray
with a bunch of dirty dishes.

53
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:32,532
And I grabbed the
knife and I stabbed him.

54
00:05:32,966 --> 00:05:34,161
How many times?

55
00:05:38,505 --> 00:05:41,031
I have no idea.

56
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:46,371
According to measurements
taken by the coroner at the scene,

57
00:05:46,546 --> 00:05:50,745
Mr. Easley was
about 6 feet tall.

58
00:05:51,017 --> 00:05:52,849
How tall are you, Miss Barrett?

59
00:05:53,153 --> 00:05:54,678
I'm 5'3".

60
00:06:10,904 --> 00:06:12,099
Ben?

61
00:06:12,272 --> 00:06:13,831
You here?

62
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:15,600
Ben?

63
00:06:15,842 --> 00:06:17,868
Go away.

64
00:06:19,179 --> 00:06:22,377
You are here. Why didn't
you answer the door?

65
00:06:23,817 --> 00:06:25,649
What are you reading?

66
00:06:27,153 --> 00:06:29,213
<i>Innocent Till Proven Dead.</i>

67
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:32,359
I read that. Yeah,
it's pretty good.

68
00:06:32,525 --> 00:06:35,962
Especially when you find
out who the murderer is.

69
00:06:36,129 --> 00:06:38,826
I figured it was the DA.

70
00:06:38,999 --> 00:06:43,164
I mean, who would have ever thought
it'd turn out to be the guy's own wife?

71
00:06:44,371 --> 00:06:47,739
- Whoops.
- Billy, you just ruined it for me.

72
00:06:47,908 --> 00:06:50,707
Well, heck, I didn't mean to.

73
00:06:50,877 --> 00:06:54,006
Leanne sent this to me all
the way from Los Angeles.

74
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:56,308
I'm sorry.

75
00:06:57,017 --> 00:06:59,145
How's she doing anyway?

76
00:06:59,319 --> 00:07:01,584
Well, pretty good. Job
seems to be working out.

77
00:07:01,755 --> 00:07:03,621
- She gonna stay?
- I imagine.

78
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:05,014
She met a fella.

79
00:07:05,191 --> 00:07:06,750
Oh.

80
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:10,122
She didn't take your spark
plug wrench with her, did she?

81
00:07:10,563 --> 00:07:12,327
- Spark plug wrench?
- Yeah, I need it.

82
00:07:12,499 --> 00:07:15,492
That's why I came over.
Truck conked out on me again.

83
00:07:17,604 --> 00:07:20,267
Did you ever consider getting
your own spark plug wrench?

84
00:07:21,274 --> 00:07:22,867
Why?

85
00:07:23,176 --> 00:07:24,337
We got yours.

86
00:07:29,849 --> 00:07:32,683
- You know you got a message?
- Yeah, I'll get it later on.

87
00:07:32,852 --> 00:07:34,377
Don't you wanna
know who it's from?

88
00:07:36,022 --> 00:07:37,786
<i>Ben, this is Ellen Barrett.</i>

89
00:07:37,958 --> 00:07:44,990
<i>I'm in Wilmington, North
Carolina, at 910-555-7000.</i>

90
00:07:45,165 --> 00:07:47,100
<i>Please call me as soon
as you get this message.</i>

91
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:49,327
<i>It's very important. Thanks.</i>

92
00:07:49,903 --> 00:07:51,599
Save that.

93
00:07:53,173 --> 00:07:54,232
Who's Ellen Barrett?

94
00:07:54,874 --> 00:07:55,967
A woman.

95
00:07:56,743 --> 00:07:58,644
- A woman?
- Yeah.

96
00:07:58,812 --> 00:08:01,077
I used to know her.
She was a reporter.

97
00:08:01,247 --> 00:08:03,910
Covered the courthouse
for the <i>Atlanta Herald.</i>

98
00:08:06,453 --> 00:08:08,319
Did you...?

99
00:08:10,190 --> 00:08:11,886
No.

100
00:08:13,226 --> 00:08:14,717
She was married.

101
00:08:14,894 --> 00:08:18,092
She has her own syndicated
column, Pulitzer Prize winner,

102
00:08:18,264 --> 00:08:21,359
and I just knew her.

103
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:24,993
Think you might be
going to Wilmington?

104
00:08:25,538 --> 00:08:27,234
I don't know.

105
00:08:28,274 --> 00:08:30,402
I got business there myself.

106
00:08:30,577 --> 00:08:34,947
My truck's on the fritz. I
might go along with you.

107
00:08:35,382 --> 00:08:37,078
Business...?

108
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:38,250
Eh...

109
00:08:38,418 --> 00:08:41,946
It just so happens that
someone's interested

110
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:44,956
in buying the house
that Lucy left me.

111
00:08:45,125 --> 00:08:46,218
Someone by the name

112
00:08:46,393 --> 00:08:47,953
- of Clarence Litton.
- Clarence Litton?

113
00:08:48,094 --> 00:08:50,723
Clarence Litton, the
billionaire with all the hotels.

114
00:08:50,897 --> 00:08:52,937
What's Clarence Litton
want with that dump of yours?

115
00:08:53,066 --> 00:08:54,546
I'll thank you not
to call it a dump.

116
00:08:54,701 --> 00:08:56,795
The roof's bad,
the wiring's shot,

117
00:08:56,970 --> 00:08:59,405
you need new plumbing,
it's termite-infested.

118
00:08:59,572 --> 00:09:00,870
I've seen roaches.

119
00:09:01,041 --> 00:09:03,101
- You have not.
- It's a dump.

120
00:09:03,276 --> 00:09:05,142
It's a prime piece of
beachfront property

121
00:09:05,311 --> 00:09:07,271
- that's gonna make me filthy rich.
- It's a dump.

122
00:09:21,361 --> 00:09:24,126
<i>Flight 584 to Charlotte</i>

123
00:09:24,297 --> 00:09:25,356
<i>now boarding at Gate 5.</i>

124
00:09:25,532 --> 00:09:26,556
Mom.

125
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:28,627
Carrie.

126
00:09:28,968 --> 00:09:30,698
Carrie, what are you doing here?

127
00:09:30,870 --> 00:09:33,169
Dad and I are staying
at the beach house.

128
00:09:33,339 --> 00:09:36,173
- Kind of a graduation present.
- Aw.

129
00:09:37,610 --> 00:09:39,476
Your hair looks
pretty that way. I like it.

130
00:09:40,346 --> 00:09:41,746
You look awful.

131
00:09:41,915 --> 00:09:43,884
Oh, I didn't sleep, that's all.

132
00:09:45,251 --> 00:09:46,947
How did you know I was here?

133
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:51,455
Well, Dad called your office
after he heard what happened.

134
00:09:51,624 --> 00:09:52,853
Well, everything is okay.

135
00:09:53,026 --> 00:09:54,654
Then why is Ben Matlock coming?

136
00:09:54,828 --> 00:09:56,956
To make sure that
everything stays okay.

137
00:09:57,130 --> 00:09:59,463
So just tell your father that...

138
00:09:59,799 --> 00:10:01,597
Tell him everything is okay.

139
00:10:02,068 --> 00:10:04,128
Why won't you let him help you?

140
00:10:04,304 --> 00:10:07,399
Oh, Carrie, your father and I are
like oil and water. We don't exactly mix.

141
00:10:07,574 --> 00:10:11,443
- He still cares about you.
- Carrie, don't involve yourself in this.

142
00:10:11,611 --> 00:10:14,103
Tell him not to
worry. I can handle it.

143
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:17,546
Come on. Now, as long as
you're here, you can meet Ben.

144
00:10:20,787 --> 00:10:22,415
Carrie!

145
00:10:25,525 --> 00:10:27,221
Carrie!

146
00:10:32,499 --> 00:10:33,660
Oh, Ellen.

147
00:10:34,434 --> 00:10:36,335
- Oh, Ben.
- Hi.

148
00:10:36,503 --> 00:10:37,943
- Good to see you.
- Good to see you.

149
00:10:38,104 --> 00:10:40,096
- It's been a long time.
- Only about seven years.

150
00:10:40,273 --> 00:10:43,300
Yeah. Long enough for you to
have acquired an entourage, I see.

151
00:10:43,476 --> 00:10:45,638
Oh, no, no, we're
just traveling together.

152
00:10:45,812 --> 00:10:47,178
This is Cliff Lewis.

153
00:10:47,347 --> 00:10:50,078
An honor to meet you. I
read your column all the time.

154
00:10:50,550 --> 00:10:52,178
And his father, Billy Lewis.

155
00:10:52,352 --> 00:10:53,513
- Hello.
- Hello.

156
00:10:53,686 --> 00:10:55,086
- Are you vacationing?
- Business.

157
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:56,787
I'm in real estate.

158
00:10:56,956 --> 00:10:59,016
In fact, I'm in the process
of hammering out a deal

159
00:10:59,192 --> 00:11:00,751
with Clarence Litton.

160
00:11:00,927 --> 00:11:03,453
- Really?
- Yeah, Clarence contacted me

161
00:11:03,630 --> 00:11:04,893
through the U.S. mail.

162
00:11:05,064 --> 00:11:07,424
- He said, "Dear Mr. Lewis..."
- You don't wanna hear this.

163
00:11:07,534 --> 00:11:09,025
Dad, Dad, Dad.

164
00:11:09,202 --> 00:11:10,922
Just because you're
selling one lousy house,

165
00:11:11,070 --> 00:11:13,904
does not give you the right to
tell people that you're in real estate.

166
00:11:14,073 --> 00:11:15,097
- Does too.
- Does not.

167
00:11:15,275 --> 00:11:16,937
Does too.

168
00:11:17,510 --> 00:11:19,911
It's not a lousy house.

169
00:11:47,006 --> 00:11:49,339
Hi. Peter Haskins.

170
00:11:49,509 --> 00:11:51,637
- Come in.
- Oh, thanks.

171
00:11:55,748 --> 00:11:57,341
Have a seat.

172
00:11:57,884 --> 00:11:59,352
Where?

173
00:12:00,486 --> 00:12:02,512
Oh, sorry.

174
00:12:03,423 --> 00:12:06,518
You are Peter
Haskins, the deputy DA?

175
00:12:06,693 --> 00:12:08,457
That's me.

176
00:12:08,828 --> 00:12:11,229
You want me to
just hold that for you?

177
00:12:11,898 --> 00:12:14,060
No. That's okay.

178
00:12:18,238 --> 00:12:19,729
Okay.

179
00:12:19,906 --> 00:12:21,568
You're here to discuss

180
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:25,303
- the Ellen Barrett case.
- That's right.

181
00:12:25,478 --> 00:12:28,175
Barrett, Barrett...

182
00:12:29,015 --> 00:12:30,483
Barrett...

183
00:12:31,985 --> 00:12:34,250
Well, why haven't you
closed her case out?

184
00:12:35,321 --> 00:12:37,654
Well, there are a
couple of inconsistencies

185
00:12:37,824 --> 00:12:40,089
in the statement
she gave the police.

186
00:12:40,727 --> 00:12:42,161
Inconsistencies?

187
00:12:42,662 --> 00:12:43,857
Yes.

188
00:12:44,030 --> 00:12:45,430
Yes. Uh...

189
00:12:45,798 --> 00:12:48,233
Ones that tend to call
into question her claim

190
00:12:48,401 --> 00:12:50,461
that she acted in self-defense.

191
00:12:50,637 --> 00:12:55,871
I can give you specifics
just as soon as I find her file.

192
00:12:56,042 --> 00:12:57,738
Is this it?

193
00:13:05,151 --> 00:13:06,983
Ahem. Okay.

194
00:13:11,524 --> 00:13:15,359
For instance, Adam
Easley, the fellow she killed,

195
00:13:16,863 --> 00:13:19,594
she says he said he
worked for Vic Baron.

196
00:13:19,766 --> 00:13:22,759
But so far, we haven't been
able to find any kind of connection

197
00:13:22,935 --> 00:13:25,063
between this Easley
and Baron at all.

198
00:13:25,538 --> 00:13:27,871
Well, if I were Vic Baron

199
00:13:28,041 --> 00:13:29,976
and was gonna have
somebody roughed up,

200
00:13:30,143 --> 00:13:34,376
I'd be sure whoever I sent to do
it couldn't be traced back to me.

201
00:13:36,416 --> 00:13:37,850
Mm.

202
00:13:39,952 --> 00:13:43,684
The second for instance,
she says Mr. Easley called her

203
00:13:43,856 --> 00:13:45,552
at her home in Atlanta
last Friday night.

204
00:13:45,725 --> 00:13:46,749
Right.

205
00:13:46,926 --> 00:13:50,419
The thing about it is he was
staying at the Sea View Motel then,

206
00:13:50,596 --> 00:13:53,464
and their records show he made
no call to her home that night

207
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:54,657
or any other night.

208
00:13:55,835 --> 00:13:57,895
Maybe he called her
from somewhere else.

209
00:13:58,504 --> 00:14:01,633
Their records do show that he
made other long distance calls

210
00:14:01,808 --> 00:14:03,037
from his motel room.

211
00:14:03,209 --> 00:14:06,043
It seems kind of strange he'd
leave just to make that one call.

212
00:14:06,512 --> 00:14:09,607
Not if he didn't want
that one call to be traced.

213
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:16,988
Your point is well
taken, Mr. Matlock.

214
00:14:17,156 --> 00:14:20,524
It could very well be
I'm nitpicking here.

215
00:14:22,695 --> 00:14:24,857
But before I charge
somebody with a crime,

216
00:14:25,031 --> 00:14:27,933
I always like to know for
sure just what that crime was.

217
00:14:28,101 --> 00:14:29,433
Yeah.

218
00:14:30,303 --> 00:14:32,143
You're not gonna lose
that file again, are you?

219
00:14:32,305 --> 00:14:35,764
Oh, no, no. I'm
gonna put it right here.

220
00:14:37,410 --> 00:14:38,434
Well, good.

221
00:14:38,611 --> 00:14:42,173
Because the sooner you
can get this cleared up,

222
00:14:42,348 --> 00:14:45,978
the sooner Ellen can
get on with her life.

223
00:14:46,152 --> 00:14:48,519
- You keep in touch.
- Oh, yes. Thank you.

224
00:14:48,688 --> 00:14:50,657
- Uh-huh. Well, I'll...
- No, no. No, sir.

225
00:14:50,823 --> 00:14:53,987
- Let me get the door for you.
- Oh, okay.

226
00:15:02,168 --> 00:15:04,865
Hon, I told you
not to go out there.

227
00:15:05,037 --> 00:15:06,596
Well, I had to do something.

228
00:15:06,773 --> 00:15:08,401
I mean, she's my mother.

229
00:15:08,574 --> 00:15:10,566
I wanted her to know
that I'm worried about her,

230
00:15:10,743 --> 00:15:12,507
that I care about her.

231
00:15:12,678 --> 00:15:16,206
She just pushed
me away like always.

232
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:19,314
It's not you, Carrie. It's me.

233
00:15:19,485 --> 00:15:21,920
I'm the one who she doesn't
want in her life anymore.

234
00:15:22,088 --> 00:15:24,819
Which is okay, I understand.

235
00:15:24,991 --> 00:15:26,323
No, it's not okay.

236
00:15:26,492 --> 00:15:28,484
Because I don't understand.

237
00:15:28,661 --> 00:15:32,257
Honey, you don't need to. This
is between your mother and me.

238
00:15:32,432 --> 00:15:34,672
Dad, if I don't understand what
happened between you two,

239
00:15:34,834 --> 00:15:37,463
how am I gonna know that the
same thing's not gonna happen to me?

240
00:15:37,837 --> 00:15:40,966
How do I know that I'm not gonna
meet somebody and fall in love

241
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:42,380
and get married and have a kid,

242
00:15:42,508 --> 00:15:44,602
only to wind up hating
my husband's guts?

243
00:15:44,777 --> 00:15:47,474
Honey, she doesn't hate my guts.

244
00:15:47,647 --> 00:15:48,876
Then what's the deal?

245
00:15:49,048 --> 00:15:50,482
<i>- Brad?</i>
- Steve?

246
00:15:50,650 --> 00:15:53,882
Yeah, Brad. What is the
holdup on the editorial?

247
00:15:56,189 --> 00:15:59,023
Look, I don't give
a damn. Just run it.

248
00:16:01,127 --> 00:16:04,029
You do not answer to him.
You answer to me, you got that?

249
00:16:04,197 --> 00:16:06,428
And we're running
that piece tonight.

250
00:16:10,236 --> 00:16:12,398
No. No, no, I wanna
see the whole page.

251
00:16:12,572 --> 00:16:14,541
Just fax it here
as soon as it's set.

252
00:16:14,707 --> 00:16:18,007
Well, that's why the paper's
paying you the big bucks, isn't it?

253
00:16:26,118 --> 00:16:27,518
Well, Ben just called.

254
00:16:27,687 --> 00:16:28,916
He talked to the DA,

255
00:16:29,088 --> 00:16:30,750
and he should be here
in about 15 minutes.

256
00:16:30,923 --> 00:16:32,083
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

257
00:16:32,225 --> 00:16:34,854
- You sure I can't get you something?
- Oh, no, thanks. I'm fine.

258
00:16:35,027 --> 00:16:36,723
You've got a nice
house here, Mr. Lewis.

259
00:16:36,896 --> 00:16:39,388
Yes, I do, don't I? It's a
real nice house. Heh, heh.

260
00:16:39,565 --> 00:16:42,626
Most people would pay a
fortune to own a place like this.

261
00:16:42,802 --> 00:16:44,896
Yes, sir, a real fortune.

262
00:16:45,071 --> 00:16:49,167
I'm a fair man, but when
something is of value, I...

263
00:16:49,342 --> 00:16:51,709
Have you ever heard the
expression don't count your chickens

264
00:16:51,878 --> 00:16:54,313
- before they're hatched?
- Clarence Litton's a billionaire.

265
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:56,540
Think he's gonna miss
a measly 500 grand?

266
00:16:56,716 --> 00:16:58,446
Five hundred grand?
What are you, nuts?

267
00:16:58,618 --> 00:17:00,678
The man's rich.
That's peanuts to him.

268
00:17:00,853 --> 00:17:02,493
I can't talk to you
when you're like this.

269
00:17:02,622 --> 00:17:05,148
- Like what?
- Delusional.

270
00:17:25,444 --> 00:17:27,606
Stop that hat!

271
00:17:34,153 --> 00:17:36,145
Oh, thanks.

272
00:17:36,322 --> 00:17:37,381
Thanks.

273
00:17:37,557 --> 00:17:39,549
Thought I was gonna
wind up swimming for it.

274
00:17:39,725 --> 00:17:42,325
Then you would've had your hands
full because I'm a lousy swimmer.

275
00:17:42,461 --> 00:17:45,056
You need to get one of those
strings that ties under your chin.

276
00:17:45,231 --> 00:17:47,257
- Or a smaller hat.
- Or a bigger head.

277
00:17:48,868 --> 00:17:50,988
You always dress like this
when you come to the beach?

278
00:17:51,137 --> 00:17:53,436
Yeah, you never know
who you're gonna meet.

279
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:55,506
I'm Jerri Stone.

280
00:17:55,875 --> 00:17:57,741
Cliff Lewis.

281
00:17:58,544 --> 00:18:01,571
- So do you live around here?
- No, Atlanta, actually.

282
00:18:01,747 --> 00:18:04,342
I'm just here helping
my father sell a house.

283
00:18:04,517 --> 00:18:06,076
It's down there.

284
00:18:06,252 --> 00:18:08,448
- On the beach?
- Yeah.

285
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:10,419
I've always wanted a
house on the beach.

286
00:18:10,590 --> 00:18:13,958
Oh, you wouldn't want that
one. It's got a lot of problems.

287
00:18:14,126 --> 00:18:15,754
How much is he asking for it?

288
00:18:15,928 --> 00:18:19,888
Well, if my father was a
normal person, he'd ask 375.

289
00:18:20,066 --> 00:18:21,986
That way, you know, he
could pay off the mortgage

290
00:18:22,134 --> 00:18:24,228
and have a little
leftover to put in the bank.

291
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:29,307
But since my father is my
father, he wants way too much.

292
00:18:29,475 --> 00:18:32,138
- Way too much?
- Way too much.

293
00:18:32,311 --> 00:18:35,713
So, what do you do?

294
00:18:35,881 --> 00:18:37,372
I'm a nurse.

295
00:18:37,550 --> 00:18:39,849
In fact, my shift
starts in an hour.

296
00:18:40,019 --> 00:18:41,681
I'm gonna have to go.

297
00:18:42,154 --> 00:18:44,282
- Thanks for saving my hat.
- You're welcome.

298
00:18:44,957 --> 00:18:47,392
- Nice meeting you.
- Nice meeting you.

299
00:18:48,894 --> 00:18:50,726
Is that Jerri with a J or a G?

300
00:18:52,632 --> 00:18:53,691
J.

301
00:18:54,700 --> 00:18:58,967
I thought maybe, you know, I
might give you a call sometime.

302
00:18:59,905 --> 00:19:01,737
That'd be nice.

303
00:19:17,556 --> 00:19:19,218
I have to stay in
town indefinitely

304
00:19:19,392 --> 00:19:21,623
just because some
deputy DA has trouble

305
00:19:21,794 --> 00:19:23,160
with some gangster's phone bill?

306
00:19:23,329 --> 00:19:25,093
That's crazy.

307
00:19:25,531 --> 00:19:27,159
Well, Peter Haskins
hasn't had a chance

308
00:19:27,333 --> 00:19:29,131
to prosecute somebody
famous before,

309
00:19:29,301 --> 00:19:31,361
so I guess he figures the
bigger he makes the case,

310
00:19:31,537 --> 00:19:32,800
the bigger office he'll get.

311
00:19:32,972 --> 00:19:35,271
I have to get back to
Atlanta. I'm on a deadline.

312
00:19:36,542 --> 00:19:39,034
You've been saying that as
far back as I can remember.

313
00:19:39,211 --> 00:19:40,907
Well, it goes
with the territory.

314
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:43,606
I thought it'd change when
you married your editor.

315
00:19:44,183 --> 00:19:45,242
Well, it didn't.

316
00:19:47,853 --> 00:19:50,118
Well, do you see
Brad at all anymore?

317
00:19:50,289 --> 00:19:51,552
No.

318
00:19:51,724 --> 00:19:54,489
He's pretty busy now
that he's editor in chief.

319
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:58,097
I have seen Carrie, though,
right here in Wilmington.

320
00:19:58,264 --> 00:20:00,358
She and Brad are spending
the summer at our...

321
00:20:00,533 --> 00:20:03,560
His beach house in Castle Cove.

322
00:20:03,736 --> 00:20:05,068
You should drop
by and say hello.

323
00:20:05,237 --> 00:20:06,237
Maybe I will.

324
00:20:10,910 --> 00:20:12,208
Hello?

325
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:16,105
Yeah.

326
00:20:16,982 --> 00:20:18,507
Yeah.

327
00:20:20,019 --> 00:20:21,783
Yeah, okay, I see.

328
00:20:21,954 --> 00:20:23,081
Yeah.

329
00:20:23,255 --> 00:20:25,724
I understand.
Thanks for the call.

330
00:20:27,193 --> 00:20:28,320
That was Mr. Haskins.

331
00:20:28,494 --> 00:20:31,794
He's found another
inconsistency in your story.

332
00:20:31,964 --> 00:20:33,330
Now what?

333
00:20:33,499 --> 00:20:36,901
Well, how did you say you got
into Adam Easley's room that night?

334
00:20:37,069 --> 00:20:40,597
I've already told them that. I
knocked on the door and he let me in.

335
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:44,433
Well, the police have found somebody
who says that's not quite the case.

336
00:20:45,544 --> 00:20:47,479
- Who?
- Ten-year-old boy,

337
00:20:47,646 --> 00:20:48,807
staying across the hall.

338
00:20:48,981 --> 00:20:51,450
He said he saw you
enter Adam Easley's room.

339
00:20:51,617 --> 00:20:52,641
But you didn't knock.

340
00:20:52,818 --> 00:20:55,481
Walked right in, and there
was no sign of Mr. Easley.

341
00:20:55,654 --> 00:20:57,145
Well, he's wrong.

342
00:20:57,323 --> 00:21:00,919
I mean, come on,
a 10-year-old kid?

343
00:21:01,093 --> 00:21:04,393
He was probably so thrilled
to be talking to real-live cops

344
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:06,243
that he'd say anything
they wanted to hear.

345
00:21:06,398 --> 00:21:08,765
It happens all the time, Ben.

346
00:21:08,934 --> 00:21:12,871
Well, all I can tell you is
Mr. Haskins believes him,

347
00:21:13,038 --> 00:21:14,158
and he thinks you're a liar.

348
00:21:14,707 --> 00:21:17,541
You lied about
Mr. Easley and Mr. Baron,

349
00:21:17,710 --> 00:21:19,030
the telephone
call, and now this.

350
00:21:19,612 --> 00:21:20,978
I didn't lie, Ben.

351
00:21:21,547 --> 00:21:22,606
He thinks you did.

352
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:43,966
Thank you very much.

353
00:21:50,643 --> 00:21:52,544
Mr. Lewis.

354
00:21:52,711 --> 00:21:56,204
- It's a pleasure to meet you.
- Feeling's mutual.

355
00:21:56,382 --> 00:21:59,011
This here is my
son, the lawyer, Cliff.

356
00:21:59,185 --> 00:22:01,586
- Yes, of course. How are you?
- Very fine, thank you.

357
00:22:01,754 --> 00:22:02,847
Well, please, have a seat.

358
00:22:03,022 --> 00:22:04,902
I take it Evelyn offered
you something to drink.

359
00:22:05,057 --> 00:22:06,923
Yeah, but I told her no.

360
00:22:07,092 --> 00:22:08,788
Told her we were
here to talk business,

361
00:22:08,961 --> 00:22:10,088
not to have a tea party.

362
00:22:11,697 --> 00:22:13,666
Well, I like your
attitude, Mr. Lewis.

363
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,468
I also like your house.

364
00:22:16,635 --> 00:22:20,970
So do I. Got a lot
of sentimental value.

365
00:22:21,140 --> 00:22:24,338
Used to belong to
my dear sister Lucy.

366
00:22:24,510 --> 00:22:26,843
She owned that house
for less than six months

367
00:22:27,012 --> 00:22:28,207
before she passed away.

368
00:22:28,380 --> 00:22:31,908
But those were the happiest
six months of her life.

369
00:22:32,151 --> 00:22:33,915
She loved that house.

370
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:38,414
The idea of somebody tearing it
down just to build something else

371
00:22:38,591 --> 00:22:44,553
like, say, a big fancy hotel...

372
00:22:45,431 --> 00:22:47,491
It would've broken her heart.

373
00:22:47,666 --> 00:22:49,635
Oh, I don't know.

374
00:22:50,102 --> 00:22:53,004
I'll bet that if I had made her the
offer that I'm about to make you,

375
00:22:53,172 --> 00:22:56,574
she would've gotten
used to the idea in no time.

376
00:23:00,846 --> 00:23:05,580
Here's what I'm prepared to
give you for that house, Mr. Lewis.

377
00:23:16,028 --> 00:23:18,896
Five hundred and
five thousand dollars?

378
00:23:19,064 --> 00:23:20,464
Yep.

379
00:23:21,133 --> 00:23:23,125
- I don't believe it.
- Oh, no.

380
00:23:23,636 --> 00:23:25,400
This is the part
you won't believe.

381
00:23:26,272 --> 00:23:28,070
I turned them down.

382
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:30,106
You turned them down?

383
00:23:30,276 --> 00:23:33,269
Come on, that's just
their first offer, Ben.

384
00:23:33,445 --> 00:23:35,437
They're bound to go higher.

385
00:23:35,614 --> 00:23:38,846
Even a country bumpkin
like you ought to know that.

386
00:23:50,195 --> 00:23:52,164
To be honest with
you, I wasn't totally sure

387
00:23:52,331 --> 00:23:54,323
I could make that
murder one charge stick,

388
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:56,162
even with the kid's testimony.

389
00:23:56,335 --> 00:23:58,304
But now that I've read
that Forensics report,

390
00:23:58,470 --> 00:23:59,995
there's not a doubt in my mind.

391
00:24:00,172 --> 00:24:03,165
Well, I don't see anything
so earth-shattering here.

392
00:24:04,043 --> 00:24:05,067
According to your client,

393
00:24:05,244 --> 00:24:07,084
Adam Easley hit her on
the head with the phone.

394
00:24:07,246 --> 00:24:09,738
- Right.
- Well, according to that report,

395
00:24:09,915 --> 00:24:12,043
only the fingerprints of
Adam Easley's right hand

396
00:24:12,217 --> 00:24:13,297
were found on the receiver.

397
00:24:13,452 --> 00:24:16,149
Thing about it is Adam
Easley was left-handed.

398
00:24:16,322 --> 00:24:18,348
Oh, come on. They
were struggling.

399
00:24:18,524 --> 00:24:20,857
Maybe he had to hold
her arm with his left hand

400
00:24:21,026 --> 00:24:23,655
and pick up the phone with
his right. He just had two.

401
00:24:23,829 --> 00:24:26,799
Maybe. But then there's the
matter of Miss Barrett's fingerprints.

402
00:24:26,966 --> 00:24:29,046
- What about them?
- If you'll turn to the next page,

403
00:24:29,201 --> 00:24:32,399
you'll see they were found on
virtually every object in that motel room.

404
00:24:32,571 --> 00:24:34,437
Which just does not
square with her contention

405
00:24:34,606 --> 00:24:37,446
she was there for a very brief
period of time before killing Mr. Easley.

406
00:24:37,576 --> 00:24:39,216
There was a commotion.
He was throwing her

407
00:24:39,345 --> 00:24:40,865
from one side of
the room to the other.

408
00:24:41,013 --> 00:24:43,013
Of course her fingerprints
were all over the place.

409
00:24:43,182 --> 00:24:44,912
Your client is
lying, Mr. Matlock.

410
00:24:45,084 --> 00:24:48,248
And the clincher is right
there on the next page.

411
00:24:50,189 --> 00:24:52,181
What next page?

412
00:25:09,274 --> 00:25:11,869
It's actually something the
investigating officer picked up

413
00:25:12,044 --> 00:25:13,979
on the night of the murder.

414
00:25:16,715 --> 00:25:21,210
Sure would like to be a fly on the wall
when Miss Barrett tries to explain that.

415
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:30,457
I scratched him
during the fight.

416
00:25:30,629 --> 00:25:32,928
He grabbed me, and
he started slapping me,

417
00:25:33,098 --> 00:25:36,535
and I remember raking
my fingers down his face.

418
00:25:36,702 --> 00:25:39,365
And then I made a run for
the door when he let me go.

419
00:25:39,538 --> 00:25:42,064
Mr. Haskins just showed me
a police report that indicates

420
00:25:42,241 --> 00:25:45,268
the scratch marks went up
Adam Easley's face, not down.

421
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:48,612
- What?
- His skin was curled up

422
00:25:48,781 --> 00:25:50,875
near his cheek,
not down by his jaw.

423
00:25:51,050 --> 00:25:52,916
Somebody went like
that instead of like that:

424
00:25:55,054 --> 00:25:57,353
I just remember clawing at him.

425
00:25:57,523 --> 00:26:00,584
I guess my fingers could
have gone up instead of down.

426
00:26:01,026 --> 00:26:05,225
Ellen, the man was a good
6, 8 inches taller than you.

427
00:26:05,397 --> 00:26:06,695
It'd be next to impossible.

428
00:26:06,999 --> 00:26:09,833
I don't care. That's
what happened.

429
00:26:10,636 --> 00:26:13,697
The police think that
you scratched his face

430
00:26:13,872 --> 00:26:16,068
after he was dead.

431
00:26:16,642 --> 00:26:18,304
Now, why would I wanna do that?

432
00:26:18,477 --> 00:26:20,912
To claim self-defense
instead of murder.

433
00:26:21,346 --> 00:26:24,646
I didn't even know Adam Easley.
Why would I wanna kill him?

434
00:26:24,817 --> 00:26:26,376
I don't know.

435
00:26:26,618 --> 00:26:28,382
Mr. Haskins
wants to talk to you.

436
00:26:29,154 --> 00:26:31,453
Talk to me or arrest me?

437
00:26:33,192 --> 00:26:34,854
I'll go with you.

438
00:26:35,027 --> 00:26:36,620
Might as well get it over with.

439
00:26:51,210 --> 00:26:52,644
Mm.

440
00:26:52,811 --> 00:26:54,643
Good choice.

441
00:26:55,013 --> 00:26:57,505
I cannot believe that I
bumped into you again.

442
00:27:00,419 --> 00:27:01,682
You look really nice.

443
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:03,454
Thank you. So do you.

444
00:27:03,956 --> 00:27:05,447
Thank you.

445
00:27:06,558 --> 00:27:08,823
So, uh, how's the
nursing business?

446
00:27:08,994 --> 00:27:12,658
Well, I'm on a floor with 47
patients and two nurses, so it's busy.

447
00:27:12,831 --> 00:27:14,766
- Yeah.
- What have you been up to?

448
00:27:15,334 --> 00:27:17,326
Oh, arguing with my pop mostly.

449
00:27:17,936 --> 00:27:19,029
Did he sell the house yet?

450
00:27:19,204 --> 00:27:22,436
Oh, remember how I told you that
he wanted too much money for it?

451
00:27:22,608 --> 00:27:25,874
- Yeah.
- Well, they offered him $505,000.

452
00:27:26,044 --> 00:27:27,273
You're kidding.

453
00:27:27,446 --> 00:27:29,506
- And he turned them down.
- You're kidding.

454
00:27:29,681 --> 00:27:32,048
No, he figured that if the buyer
wanted the house bad enough

455
00:27:32,217 --> 00:27:34,880
to offer him that much money,
he was bound to offer more.

456
00:27:35,053 --> 00:27:37,022
Well, how high does
he think the guy will go?

457
00:27:37,189 --> 00:27:38,452
Oh, he didn't say.

458
00:27:38,857 --> 00:27:40,826
My dad is, um...

459
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:44,759
Well, I can't imagine that
they'd go much more than 6.

460
00:27:44,930 --> 00:27:47,090
So if they make another
offer and it's anywhere near 6,

461
00:27:47,232 --> 00:27:49,565
I'm gonna lock him in
the room until he takes it.

462
00:27:49,735 --> 00:27:51,499
I don't blame you.
That's a lot of money.

463
00:27:52,271 --> 00:27:54,638
- It's a lot of money.
- Yeah.

464
00:27:59,111 --> 00:28:01,671
I'm really glad you
didn't have to work today.

465
00:28:03,448 --> 00:28:04,848
Me too.

466
00:28:05,651 --> 00:28:06,914
Oh, yuck.

467
00:28:07,085 --> 00:28:09,281
- Oh, does that stain?
- Oh, yeah.

468
00:28:09,454 --> 00:28:11,514
I'd better go home and
see if I can get this out.

469
00:28:11,690 --> 00:28:13,170
Oh, well, do you
want me to drive you?

470
00:28:13,325 --> 00:28:15,085
No, that's okay. My
car's right out in front.

471
00:28:15,227 --> 00:28:17,423
Well, it's not a problem.
I don't mind driving.

472
00:28:17,596 --> 00:28:19,929
We'll have to do this
some other time. I'm sorry.

473
00:28:20,098 --> 00:28:22,124
I'll see you later.

474
00:28:23,101 --> 00:28:24,660
Pardon me.

475
00:28:35,948 --> 00:28:37,416
Hi.

476
00:28:37,716 --> 00:28:39,947
Hi. What are you doing here?

477
00:28:40,452 --> 00:28:41,613
Eating.

478
00:28:42,254 --> 00:28:43,722
You're spying on me.

479
00:28:44,890 --> 00:28:46,882
Well, yeah.

480
00:28:47,826 --> 00:28:52,230
- What happened?
- Oh, she spilled wine on her skirt.

481
00:28:52,431 --> 00:28:54,161
Is she coming back?

482
00:28:54,566 --> 00:28:55,727
Probably not.

483
00:28:56,368 --> 00:28:58,701
Well, you'll see her again.

484
00:28:59,071 --> 00:29:00,437
Why do you say that?

485
00:29:01,773 --> 00:29:03,036
I don't know.

486
00:29:03,208 --> 00:29:05,768
Maybe I'm a mind
reader. Just a feeling.

487
00:29:07,913 --> 00:29:09,779
Don't look so sad.

488
00:29:16,221 --> 00:29:18,656
Of course there
are no hard feelings.

489
00:29:18,824 --> 00:29:21,555
I'm a businessman
too, don't forget.

490
00:29:22,227 --> 00:29:26,597
Yes, Mr. Lewis, I'll make sure
they validate your parking this time.

491
00:29:27,532 --> 00:29:29,000
Good.

492
00:29:29,201 --> 00:29:32,365
Well, I'll see you and your
son here tomorrow at 3.

493
00:29:32,604 --> 00:29:34,470
Okay, bye.

494
00:29:37,876 --> 00:29:39,970
You sure about this?

495
00:29:42,214 --> 00:29:44,774
You offer them
anything over $600,000,

496
00:29:44,950 --> 00:29:46,441
and I guarantee you

497
00:29:46,618 --> 00:29:49,110
Cliff will cram it down
his father's throat.

498
00:29:49,288 --> 00:29:50,312
You better be right.

499
00:29:51,390 --> 00:29:53,985
It's bad enough I
have to deal with hicks,

500
00:29:54,159 --> 00:29:56,185
but when they start
one-upping me, I...

501
00:29:56,361 --> 00:29:58,728
Well, I just don't
like it, Miss Stone.

502
00:29:58,897 --> 00:30:00,798
I just don't like that at all.

503
00:30:03,802 --> 00:30:07,239
All of them said that they
were gonna pull their ads?

504
00:30:08,473 --> 00:30:10,032
- Unless we what?
- Dad?

505
00:30:10,208 --> 00:30:12,973
No, no, no, there's no way
in hell that I'm gonna do that.

506
00:30:13,145 --> 00:30:14,636
Forget about it.

507
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:17,681
Yes, I realize that, but...

508
00:30:17,849 --> 00:30:19,647
Listen, uh, let
me think about it

509
00:30:19,818 --> 00:30:21,650
and I'll call you tomorrow,
okay? Okay, bye.

510
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:24,790
- Hi.
- Frank. Ingrid. How are you doing?

511
00:30:24,956 --> 00:30:26,796
We're doing a lot better
than you are, I think.

512
00:30:26,958 --> 00:30:29,553
Oh, well, some of our
advertisers are taking issue

513
00:30:29,728 --> 00:30:31,848
with the pro-union editorials
I've been running, so...

514
00:30:31,963 --> 00:30:34,091
I didn't just hear the
U word, did I, Ingrid?

515
00:30:34,266 --> 00:30:35,757
Cover your ears, darling.

516
00:30:35,934 --> 00:30:38,335
Frank contends that if
his mills ever went union,

517
00:30:38,503 --> 00:30:39,983
he'd be out of
business within a week.

518
00:30:42,107 --> 00:30:43,871
That's not so bad,
Frank. Think about it.

519
00:30:44,042 --> 00:30:45,602
All the water skiing
you could get done.

520
00:30:45,744 --> 00:30:48,646
Hey, you ought to see my new
boat. You saw it, didn't you, Carrie?

521
00:30:48,814 --> 00:30:51,875
- Oh, yeah, it's beautiful.
- Come on over, go for a spin.

522
00:30:52,050 --> 00:30:54,530
I'm taking her to the lake
tomorrow. It'll be your last chance.

523
00:30:54,686 --> 00:30:55,710
Yes, come over.

524
00:30:55,887 --> 00:30:59,016
I'll put on some steaks and
we'll all four have a barbecue.

525
00:30:59,191 --> 00:31:01,126
Except I don't have
anything to make salad with.

526
00:31:01,293 --> 00:31:03,888
Or any bread. And
we're all out of chips.

527
00:31:04,062 --> 00:31:06,224
Be a dear and go raid
the refrigerator, would you?

528
00:31:06,398 --> 00:31:07,889
Sure.

529
00:31:09,334 --> 00:31:14,272
Kids like her almost, almost
make me wanna have one.

530
00:31:14,439 --> 00:31:17,238
How are things between
Carrie and Ellen?

531
00:31:17,943 --> 00:31:21,072
Oh, well, it's difficult.

532
00:31:21,246 --> 00:31:25,308
Do you suppose there's something
Frank and I can do for Ellen?

533
00:31:25,917 --> 00:31:27,681
Well, I don't think so.

534
00:31:27,853 --> 00:31:29,549
Especially now.

535
00:31:29,721 --> 00:31:31,417
What do you mean?

536
00:31:34,359 --> 00:31:36,954
They just announced on the news.

537
00:31:37,763 --> 00:31:39,732
She's been arrested.

538
00:31:40,198 --> 00:31:41,791
For murder.

539
00:31:44,770 --> 00:31:46,966
Well, you're quite right
about one thing, Mr. Lewis.

540
00:31:47,139 --> 00:31:48,163
I wanna buy your house

541
00:31:48,340 --> 00:31:51,071
because I wanna tear it
down and build a hotel there.

542
00:31:51,243 --> 00:31:55,010
But if you're assuming that I'll
pay any amount of money to do so,

543
00:31:55,180 --> 00:31:56,409
you're quite wrong.

544
00:31:56,581 --> 00:32:00,575
Because I operate on a
budget like everyone else.

545
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:04,780
And this, therefore, must
be considered my final offer.

546
00:32:07,959 --> 00:32:11,293
Six hundred and
twenty thousand dollars.

547
00:32:11,463 --> 00:32:12,692
We'll take it.

548
00:32:12,864 --> 00:32:14,389
Right, Pop?

549
00:32:14,566 --> 00:32:15,966
Pop?

550
00:32:17,169 --> 00:32:19,604
- Come on, son, let's go.
- Oh, hold on.

551
00:32:19,771 --> 00:32:22,741
You just have to sign on the
line. Someone give him a pen.

552
00:32:22,908 --> 00:32:26,242
- I'm not gonna sign that.
- He just offered you $620,000.

553
00:32:26,411 --> 00:32:27,470
I heard him.

554
00:32:27,646 --> 00:32:29,945
- Let's go.
- I'm not gonna let you do this now.

555
00:32:30,115 --> 00:32:33,882
Why don't we just leave you
two alone so you can talk it over?

556
00:32:34,052 --> 00:32:35,111
No need for talk.

557
00:32:35,287 --> 00:32:37,256
I'm not taking less
than 800 and that's final.

558
00:32:37,422 --> 00:32:38,583
Eight hundred?

559
00:32:38,757 --> 00:32:41,022
- And I want the mortgage paid off too.
- What?

560
00:32:41,193 --> 00:32:43,253
Not only does that
go beyond my budget,

561
00:32:43,428 --> 00:32:45,829
it goes beyond common sense.

562
00:32:45,997 --> 00:32:48,796
I'm afraid we have nothing more
to say to each other, Mr. Lewis.

563
00:32:49,634 --> 00:32:50,966
These things happen.

564
00:32:52,671 --> 00:32:53,730
Get out the parking ticket

565
00:32:53,905 --> 00:32:56,739
so what's-her-name
out there can validate it.

566
00:32:59,544 --> 00:33:02,776
- Seven hundred thousand.
- Seven seventy-five.

567
00:33:02,948 --> 00:33:06,043
- Seven fifty.
- Plus 350 for the mortgage.

568
00:33:07,452 --> 00:33:09,318
Plus 350 for the mortgage.

569
00:33:10,489 --> 00:33:12,720
Now, you draw
that up and I'll sign it.

570
00:33:14,159 --> 00:33:16,628
Sit down, son, before you fall.

571
00:33:19,664 --> 00:33:22,828
One point one million dollars?

572
00:33:23,001 --> 00:33:25,698
That's right, Ben. You're
looking at a millionaire.

573
00:33:25,871 --> 00:33:28,670
Pop called his bluff,
the guy fell for it. I...

574
00:33:28,840 --> 00:33:31,605
Any idea what yachts
go for these days?

575
00:33:31,776 --> 00:33:34,371
Oh, what do you
care? You get seasick.

576
00:33:34,546 --> 00:33:38,381
I'll just get me a gross of those little
patches you stick behind your ear,

577
00:33:38,550 --> 00:33:41,577
and I'll be all set. Ha-ha-ha.

578
00:33:42,187 --> 00:33:45,089
Wonder what Dora-Dora's
like this time of year.

579
00:33:45,257 --> 00:33:46,725
That's Bora-Bora, Pop.

580
00:33:49,160 --> 00:33:50,958
I'll get it.

581
00:33:56,835 --> 00:34:00,966
- Hi.
- Hi, Jerri, what are you doing here?

582
00:34:01,139 --> 00:34:03,768
- Can I talk to you?
- Yeah, yeah, come in. Sure.

583
00:34:03,942 --> 00:34:06,002
Of course you can. Come on in.

584
00:34:06,311 --> 00:34:09,645
Um, Ben, Dad,
this is Jerri Stone.

585
00:34:09,814 --> 00:34:14,013
Jerri Stone, this is Ben Matlock
and my father, Billy Lewis.

586
00:34:14,185 --> 00:34:16,518
I know who you are, Mr. Lewis.

587
00:34:16,688 --> 00:34:18,714
That's why I'm here.

588
00:34:19,824 --> 00:34:22,020
I'm not a nurse.

589
00:34:22,327 --> 00:34:24,296
I'm a private investigator.

590
00:34:24,462 --> 00:34:26,363
Clarence Litton
wanted me to find out

591
00:34:26,531 --> 00:34:28,693
how much you expected
to get for this house.

592
00:34:28,867 --> 00:34:30,495
Everything I did was on purpose.

593
00:34:30,669 --> 00:34:32,831
Running into you on the beach

594
00:34:33,004 --> 00:34:35,803
and then finding out how things
stood at the restaurant last night.

595
00:34:36,441 --> 00:34:37,465
You told?

596
00:34:37,642 --> 00:34:38,905
It's okay.

597
00:34:39,744 --> 00:34:41,940
You're an unusual
man, Mr. Lewis.

598
00:34:42,747 --> 00:34:46,445
All of the information that I gave to
Mr. Litton turned out to be useless.

599
00:34:47,018 --> 00:34:48,748
Well, that's why he canned me.

600
00:34:49,588 --> 00:34:53,389
But you know what? I don't
mind because you're nice people.

601
00:34:54,859 --> 00:34:57,328
I started feeling bad
about the whole thing,

602
00:34:57,495 --> 00:35:00,226
and I just wanted to apologize.

603
00:35:00,732 --> 00:35:02,462
Especially to you.

604
00:35:02,634 --> 00:35:03,727
I'm sorry.

605
00:35:07,706 --> 00:35:09,436
That's all right.

606
00:35:09,641 --> 00:35:12,110
I've had to do some
underhanded things myself for Ben.

607
00:35:12,277 --> 00:35:14,678
I've never asked you to
do anything underhanded.

608
00:35:15,280 --> 00:35:17,010
- Sneaky?
- Sneaky, maybe,

609
00:35:17,182 --> 00:35:18,343
but not underhanded.

610
00:35:19,217 --> 00:35:20,810
Sneaky.

611
00:35:21,586 --> 00:35:22,918
Well, I gotta go.

612
00:35:23,088 --> 00:35:26,923
Hey, congratulations on
sticking it to old Litton like that.

613
00:35:27,092 --> 00:35:28,958
He thought you
were just a dumb hick.

614
00:35:29,794 --> 00:35:31,194
I know.

615
00:35:32,397 --> 00:35:34,957
- Bye.
- Bye.

616
00:35:41,373 --> 00:35:42,898
Classy lady.

617
00:35:43,074 --> 00:35:44,940
Got guts too.

618
00:35:45,110 --> 00:35:47,875
Too bad she can't hold a job.

619
00:35:54,386 --> 00:35:57,413
Bail is at set $250,000.

620
00:35:57,589 --> 00:35:58,613
As for the trial,

621
00:35:59,190 --> 00:36:01,955
it will commence the
third of next month

622
00:36:02,127 --> 00:36:03,595
at 9 a.m. in this courtroom.

623
00:36:04,996 --> 00:36:07,261
Court is adjourned till 2:00.

624
00:36:07,432 --> 00:36:08,900
This is weird.

625
00:36:09,067 --> 00:36:13,095
I've been a spectator in court a
hundred times, but sitting here...

626
00:36:13,505 --> 00:36:16,600
There were a couple dozen
reporters outside when I came in.

627
00:36:16,775 --> 00:36:18,368
You might wanna go
down the back stairs.

628
00:36:18,543 --> 00:36:20,263
- I know that's what I'm gonna do.
- Thanks.

629
00:36:20,712 --> 00:36:23,841
Figure why make their jobs,
such as they are, any easier.

630
00:36:24,015 --> 00:36:26,348
I hate reporters.

631
00:36:29,754 --> 00:36:31,552
Ellen. Yoo-hoo!

632
00:36:31,723 --> 00:36:34,022
- Ingrid.
- Oh, how are you, dear?

633
00:36:34,192 --> 00:36:35,990
We have been so
worried about you.

634
00:36:36,161 --> 00:36:38,221
- Hello, Ellen.
- Frank, hi.

635
00:36:38,396 --> 00:36:39,756
Ben Matlock, these
are my friends,

636
00:36:39,898 --> 00:36:41,258
- Frank and Ingrid Jefferies.
- Hi.

637
00:36:41,399 --> 00:36:42,479
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you.

638
00:36:42,634 --> 00:36:44,728
Are you gonna be able to
raise all that bail money?

639
00:36:44,903 --> 00:36:46,703
- Because...
- Oh, I can handle it. Thank you.

640
00:36:46,805 --> 00:36:48,885
- Thanks for offering.
- Real reason why we came here

641
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:51,441
is because we heard you're
staying in some dreadful hotel.

642
00:36:51,609 --> 00:36:52,804
Oh, well, I've been in worse.

643
00:36:52,977 --> 00:36:55,742
Well, we think you
should stay with us.

644
00:36:55,914 --> 00:36:58,611
You'd be near Carrie, and
she's very worried about you.

645
00:36:58,783 --> 00:37:00,945
- Oh, it's tempting.
- We've got an extra bedroom.

646
00:37:01,119 --> 00:37:03,884
- It seems a shame to waste it.
- Actually, it's his idea.

647
00:37:04,055 --> 00:37:06,889
- Then I accept.
- Great.

648
00:37:17,502 --> 00:37:21,064
- What's making all that racket?
- I don't know.

649
00:37:41,326 --> 00:37:43,227
Couldn't decide
between red and white

650
00:37:43,394 --> 00:37:45,022
so I got black.

651
00:37:45,196 --> 00:37:47,097
Wanna go for a ride?

652
00:37:47,265 --> 00:37:49,325
- Where did you...?
- From the bank.

653
00:37:49,701 --> 00:37:51,602
Sold a house. Collateral.

654
00:37:51,770 --> 00:37:53,898
They couldn't loan me
the money fast enough.

655
00:37:54,072 --> 00:37:56,667
Pop, deals on houses
fall through every day.

656
00:37:56,841 --> 00:37:58,707
Oh, take that
apron off and get in.

657
00:37:58,877 --> 00:38:01,437
What's he got you doing
now? Baking cookies?

658
00:38:01,613 --> 00:38:03,605
- No, we're making a stew.
- Billy.

659
00:38:03,882 --> 00:38:05,544
Jealous, huh?

660
00:38:05,717 --> 00:38:06,844
No, I'm not jealous.

661
00:38:07,018 --> 00:38:09,419
You liked it when I had to
come crawling to you all the time

662
00:38:09,587 --> 00:38:11,613
- to borrow money, didn't you?
- Are you crazy?

663
00:38:11,790 --> 00:38:15,192
Ah! The worm has turned,
and you can't stand it.

664
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:17,761
I'm just afraid the worm's
gonna do something stupid.

665
00:38:21,733 --> 00:38:24,862
<i>I'm looking over a
four-leafed clover</i>

666
00:38:25,036 --> 00:38:28,700
<i>That I overlooked before</i>

667
00:38:36,247 --> 00:38:38,409
Think it's hereditary?

668
00:39:07,512 --> 00:39:09,105
Come on.

669
00:39:15,053 --> 00:39:16,954
Be careful. Stand back.

670
00:39:17,121 --> 00:39:20,956
- Come on, get the hose.
- I told you to get the hose.

671
00:39:25,797 --> 00:39:27,197
Hey!

672
00:39:34,639 --> 00:39:36,767
Mm. Mm.

673
00:39:36,941 --> 00:39:39,604
Give me one all the way.

674
00:39:39,777 --> 00:39:41,837
Wise choice, Mr. Matlock.

675
00:39:42,013 --> 00:39:43,379
- Oh, hi.
- Hi.

676
00:39:43,548 --> 00:39:46,177
- Mind if I join you?
- Well, sure.

677
00:39:46,351 --> 00:39:48,217
Uh, give me two,
please, with the works.

678
00:39:48,386 --> 00:39:50,218
And a bag of chips...

679
00:39:50,388 --> 00:39:52,823
- Two?
- And a root beer.

680
00:39:54,592 --> 00:39:58,154
- So got a couple minutes to talk?
- Sure.

681
00:39:58,329 --> 00:40:00,059
- I was wondering...
- Oh, Mr. Matlock.

682
00:40:00,632 --> 00:40:02,123
I was just about to call you.

683
00:40:02,300 --> 00:40:05,759
- Excuse us for a moment?
- Sure.

684
00:40:10,775 --> 00:40:14,177
I've been talking to the police
up in Wallace all morning long.

685
00:40:14,345 --> 00:40:16,837
Someone tried to burn
Adam Easley's house down.

686
00:40:17,015 --> 00:40:18,950
- What?
- Last night, about midnight.

687
00:40:19,117 --> 00:40:22,178
Definitely arson, definitely
not a professional job.

688
00:40:22,353 --> 00:40:25,619
Made me think that maybe someone
who didn't have a lot of experience

689
00:40:25,790 --> 00:40:28,225
was trying to destroy
something inside the house.

690
00:40:29,460 --> 00:40:31,019
You talking about Ellen Barrett?

691
00:40:31,896 --> 00:40:33,364
Yes.

692
00:40:33,932 --> 00:40:35,924
So I had the police
search the house,

693
00:40:36,501 --> 00:40:38,436
and they found
something very curious.

694
00:40:38,603 --> 00:40:40,697
A book, written in Chinese,

695
00:40:40,872 --> 00:40:43,034
published in Shanghai
about 20 years ago

696
00:40:43,207 --> 00:40:44,732
titled <i>Birth of a Criminal.</i>

697
00:40:45,109 --> 00:40:47,203
Wasn't exactly a
bestseller even then.

698
00:40:47,378 --> 00:40:48,812
Almost impossible to get.

699
00:40:48,980 --> 00:40:51,950
And yet that's the only book
they found in that whole house.

700
00:40:52,483 --> 00:40:57,683
So I had somebody down at the
library do a quick translation, and bingo.

701
00:40:58,923 --> 00:41:01,791
Oh, sure. Keep right on going.

702
00:41:01,960 --> 00:41:04,623
What do you care, jerk?

703
00:41:07,699 --> 00:41:09,292
- Where was I?
- Bingo.

704
00:41:09,467 --> 00:41:10,560
Oh, yeah.

705
00:41:10,735 --> 00:41:12,829
- Okay. Okay?
- Yeah, I guess so.

706
00:41:13,004 --> 00:41:18,375
The book chronicles the
life of a real-life serial killer

707
00:41:19,444 --> 00:41:22,312
from his childhood
right up to the execution.

708
00:41:23,514 --> 00:41:26,074
It sounded suspiciously
similar in theme

709
00:41:26,250 --> 00:41:28,913
to that series of articles your
client did for the <i>Atlanta Herald</i>

710
00:41:29,087 --> 00:41:31,079
that won her the Pulitzer Prize.

711
00:41:31,255 --> 00:41:32,484
So I compared the two.

712
00:41:33,624 --> 00:41:35,889
They're the same, Mr. Matlock.

713
00:41:36,961 --> 00:41:40,125
I finally have what
I've been looking for.

714
00:41:40,298 --> 00:41:41,994
Motive.

715
00:41:49,207 --> 00:41:50,334
Mr. Matlock.

716
00:41:53,444 --> 00:41:55,640
You forgot your hot dog.

717
00:41:57,815 --> 00:41:59,716
- What?
- I said where were you

718
00:41:59,884 --> 00:42:01,443
between 11 and 1 last night?

719
00:42:01,619 --> 00:42:03,986
I was right here, sleeping.

720
00:42:04,489 --> 00:42:06,856
- Where were you?
- We were sleeping too.

721
00:42:07,025 --> 00:42:09,017
- In another room.
- Of course.

722
00:42:09,193 --> 00:42:11,662
So you can't say for
sure where Ellen was.

723
00:42:11,829 --> 00:42:13,957
Ben, what's this about?

724
00:42:14,132 --> 00:42:17,569
Our friend Mr. Haskins
knows about the book.

725
00:42:17,735 --> 00:42:20,967
The police in Wallace retrieved
it from Adam Easley's house

726
00:42:21,139 --> 00:42:23,233
after you tried to
burn it down last night.

727
00:42:23,408 --> 00:42:26,378
I didn't burn down
anybody's house.

728
00:42:26,544 --> 00:42:28,104
Ellen, the district
attorney has motive.

729
00:42:28,279 --> 00:42:30,373
He's gonna contend that
you killed Adam Easley

730
00:42:30,548 --> 00:42:31,982
because he was blackmailing you.

731
00:42:32,150 --> 00:42:33,516
Because he knew

732
00:42:33,684 --> 00:42:37,485
the Pulitzer Prize-winning
articles of yours were all plagiarized.

733
00:42:37,655 --> 00:42:38,918
Plagiarized?

734
00:42:39,090 --> 00:42:40,850
Ben, please, could we
do this somewhere else?

735
00:42:40,992 --> 00:42:42,153
What's the difference?

736
00:42:42,326 --> 00:42:44,659
By the end of the trial...
By the end of the day,

737
00:42:44,829 --> 00:42:46,092
everybody's gonna know.

738
00:42:46,264 --> 00:42:48,324
It's over, Ellen. So
how about the truth?

739
00:42:55,406 --> 00:42:57,807
The DA's right about the book.

740
00:42:58,242 --> 00:43:00,711
But he's wrong about me.
I didn't kill Adam Easley,

741
00:43:00,878 --> 00:43:02,574
not in self-defense
or otherwise.

742
00:43:02,747 --> 00:43:04,477
He was already dead
when I got to his room.

743
00:43:04,649 --> 00:43:07,676
Well, why did you
say you killed him?

744
00:43:09,587 --> 00:43:10,714
I can't tell you that.

745
00:43:12,123 --> 00:43:13,455
How am I gonna defend you?

746
00:43:13,624 --> 00:43:14,956
Oh, Ben, come on.

747
00:43:15,126 --> 00:43:18,096
Clients lie to their lawyers
all the time. You know that.

748
00:43:18,262 --> 00:43:20,822
Besides, I'm not lying. I
just can't tell you everything.

749
00:43:23,134 --> 00:43:24,693
Oh, Ben, come on, please.

750
00:43:24,869 --> 00:43:26,929
If you drop me, nobody
else is gonna touch me.

751
00:43:27,105 --> 00:43:28,505
I'll be convicted for sure.

752
00:43:31,843 --> 00:43:34,870
Ben, I didn't kill him.

753
00:43:52,763 --> 00:43:54,755
Well, nothing on the news.

754
00:43:55,633 --> 00:43:57,932
I guess the DA hasn't told
the press about the book.

755
00:43:58,102 --> 00:43:59,570
You know what I feel like doing?

756
00:43:59,737 --> 00:44:02,297
Remember when you and Brad
used to come over and we'd play bridge

757
00:44:02,440 --> 00:44:03,464
out there, till dawn?

758
00:44:03,641 --> 00:44:05,940
- Ingrid, they're divorced.
- I know.

759
00:44:06,444 --> 00:44:09,437
But this morning in the store
when Frank was off skiing,

760
00:44:09,614 --> 00:44:13,244
I started talking to this really
nice, very nice-looking man

761
00:44:13,417 --> 00:44:15,886
about your age, and he
mentioned he plays bridge.

762
00:44:16,354 --> 00:44:17,434
So why don't I go call him?

763
00:44:17,588 --> 00:44:19,386
Now is not the time
to be fixing her up.

764
00:44:19,557 --> 00:44:22,117
Why not? Romance strikes
when you least expect it.

765
00:44:22,460 --> 00:44:23,860
She has other
things on her mind.

766
00:44:24,028 --> 00:44:25,908
And I'm trying to get
her mind off those things.

767
00:44:26,063 --> 00:44:28,897
I know you are, Ingrid. But I
kind of feel like being alone.

768
00:44:29,066 --> 00:44:31,262
I just think I'll take a
long walk on the beach.

769
00:44:31,435 --> 00:44:34,234
- Want some company?
- No, thanks.

770
00:44:35,673 --> 00:44:37,665
Don't wait up for me.

771
00:44:39,410 --> 00:44:43,506
- Oh, Ingrid.
- What was wrong with that?

772
00:45:37,101 --> 00:45:38,660
Hello, Ellen.

773
00:45:39,270 --> 00:45:40,329
What you got there?

774
00:45:40,504 --> 00:45:41,972
Who the hell are you?

775
00:45:42,139 --> 00:45:44,819
- I'll explain to you in the car.
- I'm not going anywhere with you.

776
00:45:44,976 --> 00:45:46,842
Well, you know,
you got two choices.

777
00:45:47,011 --> 00:45:49,845
Either you can come with
me or we can call the cops.

778
00:45:50,014 --> 00:45:51,676
You decide.

779
00:46:16,107 --> 00:46:17,627
- Well, who's that?
- Probably somebody

780
00:46:17,775 --> 00:46:19,903
bringing your daddy
home in a straightjacket.

781
00:46:20,077 --> 00:46:21,170
- Hi.
- Hi.

782
00:46:21,345 --> 00:46:24,406
Mrs. Barrett would
like to talk to you.

783
00:46:25,116 --> 00:46:26,607
I'll talk to you tomorrow.

784
00:46:26,784 --> 00:46:28,150
- Bye.
- Okay, yeah.

785
00:46:31,289 --> 00:46:33,622
- What's that?
- It's blood.

786
00:46:33,791 --> 00:46:36,386
Let's sit down. This is
gonna be a long story.

787
00:46:36,560 --> 00:46:37,994
Oh.

788
00:46:41,098 --> 00:46:44,796
After, uh, I wound
up winning the Pulitzer

789
00:46:44,969 --> 00:46:48,030
for those plagiarized
articles 15 years ago,

790
00:46:48,205 --> 00:46:50,174
my conscience
started bothering me,

791
00:46:50,341 --> 00:46:52,810
and eventually I told
Brad what I'd done.

792
00:46:53,344 --> 00:46:54,744
You were still married then?

793
00:46:54,912 --> 00:46:56,847
Yeah, and he was my editor.

794
00:46:57,014 --> 00:47:00,678
I never told anybody else, but
somehow someone found out,

795
00:47:00,851 --> 00:47:04,344
and a couple of weeks
ago, a man called Brad up

796
00:47:04,522 --> 00:47:06,787
and he said that he would
make what I had done public

797
00:47:06,957 --> 00:47:10,587
unless he was given $100,000.

798
00:47:10,761 --> 00:47:13,196
He said his name
was Adam Easley.

799
00:47:14,632 --> 00:47:17,761
I wonder why he put the
squeeze on Brad instead of you.

800
00:47:17,935 --> 00:47:19,870
Oh, maybe he thought
he had more money

801
00:47:20,037 --> 00:47:22,438
or more to lose or maybe both.

802
00:47:22,606 --> 00:47:24,234
I don't know.

803
00:47:24,575 --> 00:47:28,945
Anyway, Brad told me about the threat,
and he said that he'd take care of it.

804
00:47:29,113 --> 00:47:32,277
But knowing that Brad doesn't
have the stomach for such things,

805
00:47:32,450 --> 00:47:34,146
I got worried, and I...

806
00:47:34,318 --> 00:47:36,116
At the last minute I
thought that I would go

807
00:47:36,287 --> 00:47:39,382
and meet with this Easley too.

808
00:47:40,191 --> 00:47:43,252
<i>I went to his room
and I knocked.</i>

809
00:47:43,427 --> 00:47:45,896
<i>When no one
answered, I tried the door.</i>

810
00:47:46,063 --> 00:47:47,497
<i>It was unlocked.</i>

811
00:47:47,665 --> 00:47:51,399
<i>I opened it, walked in,</i>

812
00:47:51,569 --> 00:47:56,200
<i>and there was Easley,
lying dead on the floor.</i>

813
00:47:56,374 --> 00:47:57,774
<i>The knife was next to him,</i>

814
00:47:57,942 --> 00:48:01,003
<i>and the briefcase
was underneath him.</i>

815
00:48:01,178 --> 00:48:02,544
<i>It was Brad's.</i>

816
00:48:02,713 --> 00:48:06,377
<i>I figured that Brad had
gone there, met with Easley,</i>

817
00:48:06,550 --> 00:48:10,612
<i>got mad, lost it,
and killed him.</i>

818
00:48:10,788 --> 00:48:12,450
I knew that if Brad
were arrested,

819
00:48:12,623 --> 00:48:15,218
the plagiarism would come
out and we'd be ruined.

820
00:48:15,393 --> 00:48:17,885
Brad, me, Carrie, all of us.

821
00:48:18,062 --> 00:48:19,223
I see.

822
00:48:19,397 --> 00:48:23,027
So I decided to make
it look like I'd killed him.

823
00:48:23,701 --> 00:48:27,367
<i>I went outside and I
buried the briefcase.</i>

824
00:48:27,538 --> 00:48:29,404
<i>It was empty.</i>

825
00:48:29,573 --> 00:48:32,702
<i>But I didn't want anything
of Brad's to be found.</i>

826
00:48:32,877 --> 00:48:34,869
<i>Then I went back to the room.</i>

827
00:48:35,045 --> 00:48:37,708
<i>I guess that's when
the boy saw me.</i>

828
00:48:37,882 --> 00:48:40,078
- No, I'm not gonna go!
<i>- And I called the police.</i>

829
00:48:40,384 --> 00:48:42,304
<i>And then while you were
waiting for them to come,</i>

830
00:48:42,453 --> 00:48:44,319
<i>you staged the scene.</i>

831
00:48:46,624 --> 00:48:49,059
<i>I tore my blouse,</i>

832
00:48:49,226 --> 00:48:54,665
<i>then I wiped the knife clean,
and I put my fingerprints on it.</i>

833
00:48:55,466 --> 00:48:57,697
<i>I knocked over some furniture,</i>

834
00:48:57,868 --> 00:49:00,133
<i>scratched his face.</i>

835
00:49:00,304 --> 00:49:02,864
And I whacked
myself with the phone.

836
00:49:03,040 --> 00:49:05,236
And when the police
came, you told them that lie

837
00:49:05,409 --> 00:49:07,571
about Adam Easley
working for Vic Baron.

838
00:49:07,745 --> 00:49:10,340
- Yes.
- Well, why'd you dig up the briefcase?

839
00:49:10,514 --> 00:49:12,676
So I could get rid of it.

840
00:49:13,851 --> 00:49:14,910
Brad did it.

841
00:49:16,253 --> 00:49:18,586
I think that's a
safe assumption.

842
00:49:20,357 --> 00:49:21,484
You haven't talked to him?

843
00:49:22,059 --> 00:49:25,587
We agreed not to contact each
other until after he'd talked to Easley.

844
00:49:25,763 --> 00:49:29,131
We wanted to make sure
that Carrie never found out.

845
00:49:30,901 --> 00:49:33,097
You know, that's the first
time we agreed on anything

846
00:49:33,270 --> 00:49:35,637
in almost 14 years.

847
00:49:38,008 --> 00:49:39,476
Well, to tell you the truth,

848
00:49:39,643 --> 00:49:43,580
after I overheard what the deputy
DA told you in the courthouse,

849
00:49:43,747 --> 00:49:49,448
I thought maybe I'd do a little
freelancing and follow Ellen.

850
00:49:50,387 --> 00:49:54,654
I figured if I came up with
something, maybe you'd hire me.

851
00:49:54,925 --> 00:49:56,951
That's why you came
to the courthouse?

852
00:49:57,127 --> 00:49:58,595
Yeah.

853
00:49:59,430 --> 00:50:01,194
I know you have Cliff,

854
00:50:01,365 --> 00:50:03,596
but he doesn't strike
me as the kind of person

855
00:50:03,767 --> 00:50:06,669
who's particularly good at...

856
00:50:08,372 --> 00:50:11,274
- Sneaking around?
- Yes.

857
00:50:11,876 --> 00:50:13,640
Well, he's, uh...

858
00:50:15,579 --> 00:50:18,811
The truth is I don't have
anything for you right now.

859
00:50:19,450 --> 00:50:24,411
This case, every time I
think I got a handle on it,

860
00:50:24,588 --> 00:50:27,217
you know, pull the old
rabbit out of the hat?

861
00:50:27,424 --> 00:50:29,064
I look in there and
there ain't no rabbit.

862
00:50:30,694 --> 00:50:33,687
Well, maybe I could do
some more freelancing.

863
00:50:33,864 --> 00:50:36,698
No, no. I don't like that.

864
00:50:36,867 --> 00:50:40,531
I don't want people
going behind my back.

865
00:50:41,272 --> 00:50:43,832
Especially people
who work for me.

866
00:50:51,382 --> 00:50:53,817
So Ellen thinks
that I killed him?

867
00:50:54,318 --> 00:50:55,581
You did, didn't you?

868
00:50:59,523 --> 00:51:00,616
No, no.

869
00:51:00,791 --> 00:51:04,660
Well, she found your
briefcase under the body.

870
00:51:07,498 --> 00:51:08,659
I was there that night,

871
00:51:09,433 --> 00:51:10,492
but I did not kill him.

872
00:51:10,668 --> 00:51:13,467
He was alive when I left.

873
00:51:13,637 --> 00:51:15,333
Let's hear it.

874
00:51:15,606 --> 00:51:18,075
All right. Okay.

875
00:51:22,379 --> 00:51:25,372
This guy, Easley, called
me a couple of weeks ago

876
00:51:25,549 --> 00:51:28,815
and said that he knew
Ellen had gotten the Pulitzer

877
00:51:28,986 --> 00:51:31,353
by plagiarizing that book.

878
00:51:31,822 --> 00:51:34,690
Said if I didn't pay
him 100 grand, he'd tell.

879
00:51:34,858 --> 00:51:40,593
So I got him the cash and
I met him in his room at 9.

880
00:51:45,135 --> 00:51:46,797
Is that the money?

881
00:52:07,992 --> 00:52:09,483
Wait a minute. I
don't want some copy.

882
00:52:09,660 --> 00:52:10,787
I want the book.

883
00:52:11,362 --> 00:52:13,228
The book's my insurance policy.

884
00:52:14,765 --> 00:52:16,666
How do I know you're
not gonna do this again?

885
00:52:16,834 --> 00:52:18,894
Guess you'll just
have to trust me.

886
00:52:38,022 --> 00:52:40,355
And that was it. I
went straight home.

887
00:52:40,524 --> 00:52:42,049
After Carrie went
to bed that evening,

888
00:52:42,226 --> 00:52:46,186
I threw the copy that he had given
me into the fireplace and I burned it.

889
00:52:47,931 --> 00:52:51,368
Well, the police estimate the
time of death to be about 9:30.

890
00:52:51,535 --> 00:52:54,801
Well, I was home
long before then.

891
00:52:54,972 --> 00:52:57,032
Who did you think had done it?

892
00:52:57,474 --> 00:53:00,501
Ellen. I figured she had gone
over there for some reason

893
00:53:00,678 --> 00:53:03,614
and got into a fight with the
guy and killed him in self-defense,

894
00:53:03,781 --> 00:53:05,701
- just like she said.
- What'd you think happened

895
00:53:05,849 --> 00:53:09,445
- to your briefcase and the money?
- I figured Ellen took it.

896
00:53:12,289 --> 00:53:15,282
First she said she did it, and
then she said she didn't do it,

897
00:53:15,459 --> 00:53:16,757
said you did it.

898
00:53:16,927 --> 00:53:21,058
And then you said you
didn't do it, said she did it.

899
00:53:21,231 --> 00:53:23,962
I wish you'd get
your stories straight.

900
00:53:36,780 --> 00:53:38,408
Oh, no.

901
00:53:38,615 --> 00:53:40,208
Carrie...

902
00:53:44,088 --> 00:53:46,990
Mom got that prize by cheating?

903
00:53:47,691 --> 00:53:49,956
And you knew about it.

904
00:53:51,061 --> 00:53:54,828
All those years
you've been living a lie.

905
00:53:55,566 --> 00:53:59,435
Is that why you divorced?
Because of the lie?

906
00:54:00,404 --> 00:54:03,636
You've lectured
me about honesty.

907
00:54:05,109 --> 00:54:08,170
Your honesty cost me a mother.

908
00:54:09,413 --> 00:54:11,279
- And a father.
- Carrie. Carrie.

909
00:54:11,448 --> 00:54:12,746
Carrie.

910
00:54:39,777 --> 00:54:41,268
Ellen.

911
00:54:46,016 --> 00:54:48,144
Figured I might find you here.

912
00:54:50,921 --> 00:54:54,619
- Ben Matlock came to see me today.
- I know, he told me.

913
00:54:54,792 --> 00:54:58,991
Unfortunately, Carrie was listening,
and she overheard everything.

914
00:54:59,163 --> 00:55:00,883
- What do you mean everything?
- Everything.

915
00:55:01,031 --> 00:55:05,093
Oh, for crying out loud, Brad,
how could you let that happen?

916
00:55:06,003 --> 00:55:07,972
All right, I'm sorry.

917
00:55:09,072 --> 00:55:11,940
She was very upset. She
ran out of the house in tears.

918
00:55:12,109 --> 00:55:13,304
- She ran away?
- Yeah.

919
00:55:13,677 --> 00:55:16,579
She got back an hour
ago. She's up there now.

920
00:55:16,747 --> 00:55:18,773
I've sat her down and
explained everything to her,

921
00:55:18,949 --> 00:55:21,077
and she's handling it.

922
00:55:21,251 --> 00:55:24,449
Just sitting there listening to music,
or at least what she calls music.

923
00:55:25,422 --> 00:55:27,948
Did you tell her that
stealing from that book,

924
00:55:28,125 --> 00:55:31,857
- I've regretted every day of my life?
- The best I could.

925
00:55:33,130 --> 00:55:34,393
I love her, Brad.

926
00:55:34,565 --> 00:55:39,162
The last thing in the world I want
is for her to be hurt by my stupidity.

927
00:55:40,070 --> 00:55:42,910
That's why I covered for you when I
thought that you killed Adam Easley.

928
00:55:43,040 --> 00:55:45,635
Ellen, I didn't
kill Adam Easley.

929
00:55:45,809 --> 00:55:49,143
Well, what was I supposed to think?
He was lying on top of your briefcase.

930
00:55:49,313 --> 00:55:51,077
I figured you'd killed him.

931
00:55:51,248 --> 00:55:55,310
And I knew watching you go to
prison would break Carrie's heart.

932
00:55:55,652 --> 00:55:57,746
I couldn't let that happen.

933
00:56:20,143 --> 00:56:21,975
They almost ready?

934
00:56:22,145 --> 00:56:23,511
Few seconds.

935
00:56:23,680 --> 00:56:25,760
Of course he said Adam
Easley was alive when he left.

936
00:56:25,916 --> 00:56:26,996
What else was he gonna say?

937
00:56:27,384 --> 00:56:29,478
He had to know that
incriminates Ellen.

938
00:56:29,653 --> 00:56:31,246
Well, maybe he didn't care.

939
00:56:31,421 --> 00:56:33,947
How did this Adam Easley
guy wind up with the book

940
00:56:34,124 --> 00:56:35,649
that she plagiarized?

941
00:56:35,826 --> 00:56:39,024
People familiar with
obscure Chinese literature

942
00:56:39,196 --> 00:56:41,097
generally aren't blackmailers.

943
00:56:41,265 --> 00:56:42,392
Somebody gave it to him.

944
00:56:42,933 --> 00:56:44,424
Maybe that's who killed him.

945
00:56:44,601 --> 00:56:46,365
Yeah. Hey, maybe
they were partners.

946
00:56:46,536 --> 00:56:50,473
You know, uh, instead of settling
for half the blackmail money,

947
00:56:50,641 --> 00:56:52,974
they could have wanted the
whole thing so they killed him.

948
00:56:53,143 --> 00:56:55,840
You know, there's
still 100 grand missing.

949
00:56:56,980 --> 00:56:59,506
Okay, here you go.

950
00:56:59,683 --> 00:57:02,585
- Blueberry pancakes.
- Oh, yes.

951
00:57:02,753 --> 00:57:04,949
- Oh, yes.
- Good.

952
00:57:08,091 --> 00:57:10,959
I think we should go for
this Adam Easley guy.

953
00:57:11,862 --> 00:57:14,832
Well, the police say that he
owed two months back rent

954
00:57:14,998 --> 00:57:18,332
on the house in Wallace, and he
had no visible means of support.

955
00:57:18,502 --> 00:57:20,937
Hadn't filed an income
tax return in five years,

956
00:57:21,104 --> 00:57:23,903
and, at this point, no one's
come forward to claim his body.

957
00:57:24,074 --> 00:57:26,100
It's hard to know
where to begin.

958
00:57:26,276 --> 00:57:27,300
How about with him?

959
00:57:43,827 --> 00:57:45,921
People who can eat
from morning to night

960
00:57:46,096 --> 00:57:48,429
without getting
fat drive me crazy.

961
00:58:03,013 --> 00:58:04,845
Thanks a lot.

962
00:58:05,282 --> 00:58:08,946
So this is the
infamous Adam Easley.

963
00:58:09,119 --> 00:58:12,089
Medical examiner did a
real number on him, huh?

964
00:58:12,255 --> 00:58:15,748
Looks like he put everything
back pretty much in the right place.

965
00:58:16,860 --> 00:58:18,954
- You okay?
- Mm.

966
00:58:19,596 --> 00:58:21,792
Haven't you ever
done this before?

967
00:58:22,466 --> 00:58:24,833
Oh, you should've
said something.

968
00:58:25,002 --> 00:58:27,369
I used to do this all the
time when I was a cop.

969
00:58:27,537 --> 00:58:29,938
I always thought it was so cool
how you could learn so much

970
00:58:30,107 --> 00:58:32,838
from people just by
looking at their corpses.

971
00:58:33,043 --> 00:58:34,602
For instance,
look at that liver.

972
00:58:34,778 --> 00:58:36,440
See how kind of puffy it is?

973
00:58:36,613 --> 00:58:39,447
Beginning stages of cirrhosis.

974
00:58:39,616 --> 00:58:40,982
This guy liked to drink.

975
00:58:42,386 --> 00:58:44,446
Sure was tan.

976
00:58:46,490 --> 00:58:47,958
Take a look at this.

977
00:58:48,125 --> 00:58:51,254
Calluses all across
his palm. See that?

978
00:58:51,428 --> 00:58:52,828
Hmm.

979
00:58:54,798 --> 00:58:55,822
Left hand too.

980
00:58:57,067 --> 00:59:00,560
Look at that. Tan
line on his ring finger.

981
00:59:00,737 --> 00:59:03,366
Was he wearing a ring
when they bagged him?

982
00:59:03,607 --> 00:59:06,133
Watch, wallet, gold
necklace. No rings.

983
00:59:06,309 --> 00:59:07,402
Huh.

984
00:59:07,577 --> 00:59:08,817
Well, if it was a wedding band,

985
00:59:08,945 --> 00:59:12,177
looks like he was wearing
it till pretty recently.

986
00:59:13,784 --> 00:59:16,413
Maybe there's an
ex-wife somewhere.

987
00:59:16,887 --> 00:59:19,618
Maybe she's the one
with all the answers.

988
00:59:37,941 --> 00:59:39,705
Mom!

989
00:59:41,511 --> 00:59:43,707
Oh, Carrie.

990
00:59:48,485 --> 00:59:50,613
How could you do it?

991
00:59:52,722 --> 00:59:56,489
How many countless times
I've asked myself that question.

992
00:59:57,194 --> 01:00:04,260
I was young, I was ambitious,
and incredibly stupid.

993
01:00:05,368 --> 01:00:08,497
And you thought no
one would ever find out?

994
01:00:09,873 --> 01:00:12,399
Well, I thought that
I could live with it.

995
01:00:12,576 --> 01:00:15,307
And in time, I thought I had to.

996
01:00:15,479 --> 01:00:18,608
I hope you can forgive
me someday, Carrie.

997
01:00:20,350 --> 01:00:24,151
I was dishonest, I
lied, I cheated, I stole,

998
01:00:24,321 --> 01:00:25,345
and I lived with it.

999
01:00:25,522 --> 01:00:27,650
And I made your
dad live with it.

1000
01:00:27,824 --> 01:00:30,350
That's what ruined our marriage.

1001
01:00:31,328 --> 01:00:34,025
I lost him and I lost you.

1002
01:00:35,298 --> 01:00:37,824
You didn't lose me.

1003
01:00:38,101 --> 01:00:39,569
You just didn't want me.

1004
01:00:39,736 --> 01:00:42,205
Oh, Carrie, no.

1005
01:00:42,372 --> 01:00:47,709
I didn't fight for custody of you
because I thought I didn't deserve you.

1006
01:01:24,748 --> 01:01:26,614
Mr. Haskins.

1007
01:01:27,984 --> 01:01:29,213
Mr. Haskins!

1008
01:01:39,629 --> 01:01:41,530
Ideally,

1009
01:01:44,801 --> 01:01:49,296
what you hear in a
court of law is the truth.

1010
01:01:51,608 --> 01:01:53,839
But I'm afraid that what
you're gonna hear mostly

1011
01:01:54,010 --> 01:01:58,141
in this court of law are lies.

1012
01:02:00,217 --> 01:02:06,157
You will hear how Ellen
Barrett told the police

1013
01:02:06,323 --> 01:02:09,521
that she went to Adam
Easley's hotel room

1014
01:02:09,693 --> 01:02:12,219
to get information on
a suspected mobster

1015
01:02:12,395 --> 01:02:14,261
named Vic Baron.

1016
01:02:15,031 --> 01:02:18,559
You will hear how Adam Easley
greeted her at the door as a friend

1017
01:02:18,735 --> 01:02:20,829
and how chagrinned she was

1018
01:02:21,004 --> 01:02:23,701
when moments later he told
her he'd been sent by Vic Baron

1019
01:02:23,873 --> 01:02:25,569
to beat her up.

1020
01:02:27,244 --> 01:02:29,577
You will hear how she...

1021
01:02:29,846 --> 01:02:31,508
How he.

1022
01:02:31,848 --> 01:02:33,441
Pulled her hair

1023
01:02:34,117 --> 01:02:37,576
and he tore her clothes
and he hit her on the head,

1024
01:02:37,754 --> 01:02:42,249
and how she, in turn,
scratched his face

1025
01:02:43,360 --> 01:02:46,694
and ultimately stabbed him
to death with a steak knife.

1026
01:02:48,932 --> 01:02:50,457
But these will
have all been lies.

1027
01:02:50,834 --> 01:02:53,133
Because you will then
hear how no connection

1028
01:02:53,303 --> 01:02:57,900
between Adam Easley and
Vic Baron has ever been found,

1029
01:02:58,375 --> 01:03:01,038
how in all likelihood,

1030
01:03:01,211 --> 01:03:03,874
the injuries she suffered
were self-inflicted,

1031
01:03:04,047 --> 01:03:07,245
and how the scratch
mark on the victim

1032
01:03:07,417 --> 01:03:09,613
was put there after he was dead.

1033
01:03:10,320 --> 01:03:13,415
You will hear how Miss
Barrett tried to make it look

1034
01:03:13,590 --> 01:03:16,219
as if she murdered Adam
Easley in self-defense,

1035
01:03:16,393 --> 01:03:20,990
when in point of fact, it
was premeditated murder.

1036
01:03:24,801 --> 01:03:26,861
Why did she kill him?

1037
01:03:28,438 --> 01:03:31,101
Ladies and gentlemen,
couched in that answer

1038
01:03:31,274 --> 01:03:33,675
is the biggest lie of all.

1039
01:03:35,378 --> 01:03:37,813
Because you will
hear how Ellen Barrett,

1040
01:03:37,981 --> 01:03:40,541
famous reporter and columnist,

1041
01:03:40,717 --> 01:03:43,551
renowned for the series of
articles she wrote years ago

1042
01:03:43,720 --> 01:03:46,622
that won her the Pulitzer Prize,

1043
01:03:46,790 --> 01:03:50,158
how she was being
blackmailed by Adam Easley.

1044
01:03:50,327 --> 01:03:51,761
Why?

1045
01:03:51,928 --> 01:03:54,796
Because he found out
that that series of articles

1046
01:03:54,964 --> 01:03:57,524
had, in fact, been
written by somebody else.

1047
01:03:57,701 --> 01:04:00,694
Ladies and gentlemen,
Adam Easley is dead

1048
01:04:00,870 --> 01:04:05,137
because he found out that
Ellen Barrett is a plagiarist.

1049
01:04:31,868 --> 01:04:33,302
- Found him.
- Ah.

1050
01:04:33,470 --> 01:04:34,950
Are you sure I can't
get you anything?

1051
01:04:35,105 --> 01:04:37,199
Oh, no, I'm fine. Thank you.

1052
01:04:37,374 --> 01:04:39,054
- Is this you in all these pictures?
- Yep.

1053
01:04:39,209 --> 01:04:41,542
Frank skis
practically every day.

1054
01:04:41,711 --> 01:04:43,511
Even when he's making
the rounds of his mills,

1055
01:04:43,646 --> 01:04:45,376
he comes home by
way of Lake George.

1056
01:04:45,548 --> 01:04:48,245
But I don't mind. I like
what it does for his pecs.

1057
01:04:48,418 --> 01:04:49,647
- Ingrid.
- Ha, ha, ha.

1058
01:04:49,819 --> 01:04:51,014
What can we do for you?

1059
01:04:51,187 --> 01:04:56,319
Well, I'm just still trying to
get a handle on this case.

1060
01:04:56,659 --> 01:04:58,127
Think...

1061
01:04:58,862 --> 01:05:00,797
Did you happen to
be home that night?

1062
01:05:00,964 --> 01:05:02,057
I wasn't.

1063
01:05:02,232 --> 01:05:04,133
Tuesday is ladies'
night at the racquet club.

1064
01:05:04,300 --> 01:05:05,324
I was home.

1065
01:05:06,035 --> 01:05:08,004
Uh, you remember
if Brad was home?

1066
01:05:08,171 --> 01:05:09,571
Is that what you're doing?

1067
01:05:09,739 --> 01:05:11,207
You're checking up on Brad.

1068
01:05:11,975 --> 01:05:14,501
Well, you're a friend of his,
and he lives across the street.

1069
01:05:14,677 --> 01:05:18,205
And like they say, the
truth never hurts, so I just...

1070
01:05:20,049 --> 01:05:21,711
Well, I know he was home at 11

1071
01:05:21,885 --> 01:05:23,925
because there was something
about Ellen on the news,

1072
01:05:24,087 --> 01:05:26,022
and I called him
right away to tell him.

1073
01:05:26,189 --> 01:05:27,851
Uh-huh, uh-huh.

1074
01:05:28,024 --> 01:05:30,084
You remember
seeing him before that?

1075
01:05:30,794 --> 01:05:32,262
Yes, as a matter of fact, I do.

1076
01:05:32,429 --> 01:05:35,422
I saw him pull into the driveway. I
remember because his headlights

1077
01:05:35,598 --> 01:05:37,478
shine right into our house
whenever he pulls in.

1078
01:05:37,634 --> 01:05:40,934
Just between you, me and the
lamppost, it's really annoying.

1079
01:05:41,771 --> 01:05:44,301
Uh... When would that have been?

1080
01:05:44,474 --> 01:05:47,376
Well, seems to me I was
watching <i>Inside the FBI</i> at the time.

1081
01:05:47,544 --> 01:05:50,139
- He never misses that show.
- So it must have been sometime

1082
01:05:50,313 --> 01:05:51,713
between 9 and 9:30.

1083
01:05:53,383 --> 01:05:57,946
Well, I had a feeling
Brad didn't do it.

1084
01:05:59,222 --> 01:06:00,485
Well, who do you think did?

1085
01:06:01,858 --> 01:06:05,488
Oh, well, uh, I'm
damned if I know.

1086
01:06:38,261 --> 01:06:39,456
There's a dog in here.

1087
01:06:42,866 --> 01:06:44,835
Mrs. Easley!

1088
01:06:45,001 --> 01:06:46,867
Are you in there?

1089
01:06:47,036 --> 01:06:48,868
Hello?

1090
01:07:00,350 --> 01:07:01,579
You shouldn't torment the dog.

1091
01:07:01,751 --> 01:07:04,880
Hey, if she's in there, she's gonna
come out and see what's going on.

1092
01:07:13,663 --> 01:07:15,495
Hush up.

1093
01:07:24,607 --> 01:07:28,476
I told them yesterday I wasn't
gonna have any money till Friday.

1094
01:07:28,678 --> 01:07:31,273
No, whoa, whoa, we
don't want any money.

1095
01:07:31,447 --> 01:07:33,973
We just wanna talk to
you about your ex-husband.

1096
01:07:35,518 --> 01:07:37,248
Bum's dead. What's to ask?

1097
01:07:38,154 --> 01:07:39,588
When was the last
time you saw him?

1098
01:07:41,090 --> 01:07:42,649
About a week before he died.

1099
01:07:42,825 --> 01:07:44,691
Was that here or up in Wallace?

1100
01:07:46,095 --> 01:07:47,825
Lake George.

1101
01:07:47,997 --> 01:07:50,364
He just about lived out there.

1102
01:07:50,533 --> 01:07:53,469
Kept the place in Wallace
just so he could take a shower

1103
01:07:53,636 --> 01:07:55,127
once in a while.

1104
01:07:55,305 --> 01:07:57,425
I went up there to tell him
that the divorce was final

1105
01:07:57,574 --> 01:08:00,373
and that he owed me
four months' child support.

1106
01:08:00,610 --> 01:08:01,873
Told him I'd gotten a lawyer

1107
01:08:02,045 --> 01:08:03,843
and was gonna go
after his lousy ski boat

1108
01:08:04,013 --> 01:08:05,072
if he didn't pay up.

1109
01:08:05,248 --> 01:08:07,080
A water ski boat?

1110
01:08:07,250 --> 01:08:08,741
That's why we
never had any money.

1111
01:08:09,485 --> 01:08:11,454
It all went into
hanging around the lake

1112
01:08:11,621 --> 01:08:14,284
trying to impress those
rich dudes out there.

1113
01:08:14,457 --> 01:08:17,291
Parties, beer, wine, whiskey.

1114
01:08:17,460 --> 01:08:20,794
That damn ski
boat, the <i>Candy Man.</i>

1115
01:08:22,865 --> 01:08:26,165
Anyway, Adam said he'd have
the money for me in a week.

1116
01:08:26,336 --> 01:08:28,498
Then the bum goes and dies,

1117
01:08:28,671 --> 01:08:31,937
and here I am hiding in the bathroom
every time there's a knock on the door

1118
01:08:32,108 --> 01:08:33,599
for fear that it's
a bill collector.

1119
01:08:33,776 --> 01:08:36,405
Did he say where he
was gonna get the money?

1120
01:08:37,347 --> 01:08:38,645
He didn't say.

1121
01:08:38,815 --> 01:08:40,841
I didn't ask.

1122
01:08:41,351 --> 01:08:44,651
Adam wasn't a man what you'd
call had a whole lot of principles.

1123
01:09:01,504 --> 01:09:03,473
Oh, hello. I'm Clarence Litton.

1124
01:09:03,640 --> 01:09:04,972
Is Mr. Lewis here?

1125
01:09:05,141 --> 01:09:07,770
- Oh, yes, come on in, please.
- Thank you.

1126
01:09:08,244 --> 01:09:09,644
Here.

1127
01:09:10,146 --> 01:09:12,047
- Billy?
- What?

1128
01:09:12,215 --> 01:09:13,239
Billy?

1129
01:09:13,416 --> 01:09:15,044
What?

1130
01:09:16,219 --> 01:09:17,983
Hello, Mr. Lewis.

1131
01:09:18,321 --> 01:09:21,758
I stopped by because
I'm afraid there's...

1132
01:09:21,924 --> 01:09:23,790
Well, there's a small problem,

1133
01:09:23,960 --> 01:09:26,623
and I'd like to explain
it to you personally.

1134
01:09:31,534 --> 01:09:33,332
You're not gonna buy the house?

1135
01:09:33,503 --> 01:09:36,337
Yes, we're going
to buy the house.

1136
01:09:36,939 --> 01:09:39,704
But, unfortunately, we're
not gonna be able to do so

1137
01:09:39,876 --> 01:09:42,505
- as soon as we'd planned.
- What do you mean?

1138
01:09:43,046 --> 01:09:45,675
Oh, due to some mix-up
in our Legal Department,

1139
01:09:45,848 --> 01:09:48,647
we're not gonna be able to pay
off the mortgage until Thursday,

1140
01:09:48,818 --> 01:09:50,650
not tomorrow as we agreed.

1141
01:09:50,820 --> 01:09:53,619
- Thursday? This Thursday?
- Correct, correct.

1142
01:09:54,257 --> 01:09:56,886
But in the meantime,
I brought by a check

1143
01:09:57,060 --> 01:09:58,722
for what we agreed to pay you,

1144
01:09:58,895 --> 01:10:03,799
well, in hopes that it would help
you accept our profound apologies

1145
01:10:04,600 --> 01:10:06,068
for the slight delay.

1146
01:10:08,438 --> 01:10:10,498
Yeah, okay.

1147
01:10:10,673 --> 01:10:13,142
Of course I'll have to
run this Thursday thing

1148
01:10:13,309 --> 01:10:15,210
by my son the lawyer,
see what he says.

1149
01:10:15,378 --> 01:10:16,505
Oh, of course, of course.

1150
01:10:16,679 --> 01:10:18,614
And if he has any
questions, Mr. Lewis,

1151
01:10:18,781 --> 01:10:20,647
just have him call me
and we'll discuss this...

1152
01:10:20,817 --> 01:10:21,897
- Always a pleasure.
- Good.

1153
01:10:22,051 --> 01:10:24,131
- Always a pleasure.
- Thank you, Mr. Lewis. Goodbye.

1154
01:10:24,287 --> 01:10:25,550
Let me see that.

1155
01:10:26,222 --> 01:10:28,953
Seven hundred and
fifty thousand smackers.

1156
01:10:30,326 --> 01:10:33,854
I'll just parlay that
into a couple of million.

1157
01:10:46,008 --> 01:10:48,910
Now, water skiing, that could
explain Adam Easley's dark tan.

1158
01:10:49,078 --> 01:10:51,741
- And the calluses on his hands.
- Yeah.

1159
01:10:51,914 --> 01:10:53,940
You know, I think this
is gonna work out good.

1160
01:10:54,117 --> 01:10:55,642
- What?
- Well, you and me

1161
01:10:55,818 --> 01:10:57,013
teaming up like this.

1162
01:10:57,186 --> 01:10:59,155
You wanna have
some dinner tonight?

1163
01:10:59,322 --> 01:11:00,688
Oh, Cliff, I don't think...

1164
01:11:00,857 --> 01:11:03,793
You don't think I'm your type,
right? I... That's all right, I understand.

1165
01:11:03,960 --> 01:11:06,122
Well, it's just that I recently
broke up with somebody

1166
01:11:06,295 --> 01:11:08,890
and I'm not really ready to get
involved with somebody else.

1167
01:11:09,065 --> 01:11:11,125
- You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

1168
01:11:12,235 --> 01:11:13,669
Good.

1169
01:11:14,070 --> 01:11:16,972
I think I saw a phone over by that
gas station. I'm gonna go call Ben.

1170
01:11:18,141 --> 01:11:20,872
I'll, uh, just meet
you by the boats.

1171
01:11:21,043 --> 01:11:22,534
Okay.

1172
01:11:23,179 --> 01:11:25,080
I'll see you over there then.

1173
01:12:26,008 --> 01:12:30,139
This ain't your boat, pal.
You the one that's been

1174
01:12:30,313 --> 01:12:32,475
- ripping everybody off down here?
- No. No.

1175
01:12:32,648 --> 01:12:35,117
You the one that got
my radio last week?

1176
01:12:37,286 --> 01:12:38,447
Come here.

1177
01:12:38,621 --> 01:12:39,987
This is not your boat.

1178
01:12:40,156 --> 01:12:41,180
I know that, I know that.

1179
01:12:41,357 --> 01:12:43,758
If you'll give me one
moment, I'll explain.

1180
01:12:43,926 --> 01:12:45,451
Help! Boat loose!

1181
01:12:47,230 --> 01:12:48,459
Somebody help me!

1182
01:12:53,369 --> 01:12:54,393
Oh.

1183
01:12:54,570 --> 01:12:55,833
- You got it?
- Yeah, I got it.

1184
01:12:56,005 --> 01:12:57,667
Oh, good.

1185
01:12:58,541 --> 01:13:00,567
Yeah, hold on. Bring it around.

1186
01:13:03,145 --> 01:13:04,545
Yeah.

1187
01:13:07,116 --> 01:13:09,642
- This yours?
- Yeah. Thanks, I owe you one.

1188
01:13:10,152 --> 01:13:11,984
- Don't mention it.
- No, really.

1189
01:13:12,154 --> 01:13:13,588
How about I buy you a drink?

1190
01:13:15,658 --> 01:13:17,251
Sure.

1191
01:13:24,867 --> 01:13:26,233
Yeah.

1192
01:13:31,440 --> 01:13:32,533
Mr. Matlock?

1193
01:13:34,310 --> 01:13:35,334
Hi.

1194
01:13:35,778 --> 01:13:37,474
I am Carrie Gelardi.

1195
01:13:37,647 --> 01:13:39,013
I was coming to see you.

1196
01:13:39,181 --> 01:13:43,551
Oh. The last time I saw you,
you were as big as a grasshopper.

1197
01:13:46,255 --> 01:13:48,451
It doesn't look very
good for my mom, does it?

1198
01:13:48,958 --> 01:13:50,483
Um...

1199
01:13:51,027 --> 01:13:52,620
- Well...
- I hope you're not gonna try

1200
01:13:52,795 --> 01:13:54,093
and pin it on my dad.

1201
01:13:54,630 --> 01:13:56,189
Well, to tell you the truth...

1202
01:13:56,365 --> 01:13:58,925
Because he was telling
the truth, Mr. Matlock.

1203
01:13:59,101 --> 01:14:01,570
He was home at 9:15. I
know because of my car.

1204
01:14:02,471 --> 01:14:03,769
- Your car?
- Yeah.

1205
01:14:03,940 --> 01:14:06,102
I parked my car in the driveway

1206
01:14:06,275 --> 01:14:08,506
and when I park my
car in the driveway,

1207
01:14:08,678 --> 01:14:10,442
he can't park his
car in the garage.

1208
01:14:10,613 --> 01:14:14,209
Oh. Well, did you move
your car so he could...?

1209
01:14:14,383 --> 01:14:16,682
No. No, he was too upset.

1210
01:14:16,852 --> 01:14:21,756
He was so upset he said forget
it and left his car in the street.

1211
01:14:21,924 --> 01:14:24,758
I'm really worried
about the both of them.

1212
01:14:26,462 --> 01:14:29,159
Well, I...

1213
01:14:29,432 --> 01:14:32,891
Well, thank you
for the information.

1214
01:14:33,235 --> 01:14:34,259
I'll see you later.

1215
01:14:54,357 --> 01:14:56,258
What say, Frank?

1216
01:14:56,692 --> 01:14:59,423
Mr. Matlock, what's up?

1217
01:14:59,595 --> 01:15:02,724
Remember you told me
how you saw Brad's headlights

1218
01:15:02,898 --> 01:15:04,867
when he turned
into his driveway?

1219
01:15:05,034 --> 01:15:07,003
- Yeah.
- I just found out

1220
01:15:07,169 --> 01:15:10,037
that he didn't turn into
his driveway that night.

1221
01:15:10,373 --> 01:15:12,467
So you couldn't have
seen his headlights.

1222
01:15:15,444 --> 01:15:16,969
You caught me.

1223
01:15:17,613 --> 01:15:18,808
Why'd you lie?

1224
01:15:20,583 --> 01:15:22,518
I've known Brad for 20 years.

1225
01:15:22,685 --> 01:15:25,348
He came to my wedding. I
saw him through his divorce.

1226
01:15:25,521 --> 01:15:27,490
There's no way in hell
he could kill anyone.

1227
01:15:27,656 --> 01:15:29,249
He's a good guy.

1228
01:15:29,425 --> 01:15:31,394
I was just trying to
protect him, that's all.

1229
01:15:32,928 --> 01:15:34,487
Maybe what I did was wrong,

1230
01:15:34,864 --> 01:15:36,833
but I did it for
the right reason.

1231
01:15:36,999 --> 01:15:38,763
I'm sorry.

1232
01:15:41,037 --> 01:15:42,733
Okay.

1233
01:15:51,147 --> 01:15:52,911
What were you doing
out at Lake George?

1234
01:15:53,082 --> 01:15:55,881
Oh, Adam Easley's ex-wife said
that he was out there water skiing

1235
01:15:56,052 --> 01:15:57,172
about a week before he died,

1236
01:15:57,319 --> 01:15:59,948
and that he was about to
come into a lot of money.

1237
01:16:00,122 --> 01:16:02,057
Uh, so we went out
there, we found his boat.

1238
01:16:02,625 --> 01:16:05,823
Had a little run-in with his dock mate
who's very strong and not very nice.

1239
01:16:05,995 --> 01:16:07,258
Oh, he was nice to me.

1240
01:16:07,430 --> 01:16:09,262
He bought me a drink.

1241
01:16:09,732 --> 01:16:11,166
Right.

1242
01:16:11,333 --> 01:16:14,326
Anyway, uh, we came
up empty-handed.

1243
01:16:16,572 --> 01:16:19,736
Did you say Adam Easley
water skis out at Lake George?

1244
01:16:20,543 --> 01:16:22,512
- All the time.
- Well, that's interesting

1245
01:16:22,678 --> 01:16:25,648
because Brad Gelardi's next
door neighbor, Frank Jefferies,

1246
01:16:25,815 --> 01:16:26,839
does too.

1247
01:16:40,262 --> 01:16:41,730
Hmm.

1248
01:16:43,199 --> 01:16:47,796
<i>Can she bake a cherry
pie Billy Boy, Billy Boy?</i>

1249
01:16:47,970 --> 01:16:52,567
<i>Can she bake a cherry
pie Charming Billy?</i>

1250
01:16:52,741 --> 01:16:56,371
<i>She can bake a cherry pie
She's the apple of my eye</i>

1251
01:16:56,545 --> 01:16:59,310
<i>She's a young thing And
cannot leave her mother</i>

1252
01:16:59,482 --> 01:17:02,179
Hey, I'm gonna have
to tune up the old guitar.

1253
01:17:02,351 --> 01:17:03,876
Yeah. I got some good news.

1254
01:17:04,053 --> 01:17:06,420
- What?
- Well, I went back out to the lake,

1255
01:17:06,589 --> 01:17:09,821
and I showed the picture of
Frank Jefferies to my new pal.

1256
01:17:09,992 --> 01:17:11,460
- Did he recognize him?
- Yep.

1257
01:17:11,627 --> 01:17:13,789
Didn't know who he
was, but he did say

1258
01:17:13,963 --> 01:17:16,083
that he'd seen him with
Adam Easley a couple of times.

1259
01:17:16,232 --> 01:17:19,293
- Oh, then we're on the right track.
- Mm-hm.

1260
01:17:19,468 --> 01:17:22,302
- How's it going here?
- Eh.

1261
01:17:23,706 --> 01:17:26,676
Wonder what happened
to all that money.

1262
01:17:40,890 --> 01:17:42,984
Here's a passbook.

1263
01:17:47,429 --> 01:17:50,593
Well, the last big deposit was
made two months before the murder.

1264
01:17:50,766 --> 01:17:52,826
That won't help us.

1265
01:17:53,002 --> 01:17:55,597
Come on now, come on.
Children, not on the carpet.

1266
01:17:55,771 --> 01:17:57,399
I just got them shampooed.

1267
01:17:57,573 --> 01:17:59,405
Pizza will be here any minute.

1268
01:17:59,575 --> 01:18:01,271
Pizza! Pizza!

1269
01:18:01,443 --> 01:18:03,173
Pizza!

1270
01:18:03,812 --> 01:18:06,338
Your maid let us
in, Miss Jefferies.

1271
01:18:06,515 --> 01:18:09,041
We have a warrant
to search the premises.

1272
01:18:09,952 --> 01:18:11,853
Back outside. Come on, let's go.

1273
01:18:12,021 --> 01:18:13,284
Let's go. Now, come on.

1274
01:18:13,455 --> 01:18:14,946
All of you, all of you.

1275
01:18:15,124 --> 01:18:16,990
- Right now, right now.
- Golly.

1276
01:18:17,159 --> 01:18:19,424
Or no pizza.

1277
01:18:22,965 --> 01:18:24,399
What are you doing here?

1278
01:18:25,000 --> 01:18:27,902
Well, just checking bank
statements and such.

1279
01:18:28,070 --> 01:18:30,096
Like I say, we have a warrant.

1280
01:18:30,673 --> 01:18:31,971
Are Frank and I suspects?

1281
01:18:32,441 --> 01:18:34,535
They're just
covering all the bases.

1282
01:18:34,710 --> 01:18:35,734
Who are the kids?

1283
01:18:36,478 --> 01:18:37,673
Underprivileged children.

1284
01:18:37,846 --> 01:18:40,077
I throw a beach party
for them every summer.

1285
01:18:40,449 --> 01:18:41,940
That's nice.

1286
01:18:42,117 --> 01:18:45,986
Well, we'll be going,
and have a nice party.

1287
01:18:56,131 --> 01:18:57,997
Pizza's here, Mrs. Jefferies.

1288
01:18:58,167 --> 01:19:00,033
- Excuse us.
- Sorry.

1289
01:19:02,004 --> 01:19:04,735
- I'm sorry, I can't break a hundred.
- You're kidding.

1290
01:19:04,907 --> 01:19:08,173
- Why not? What am I supposed...?
- I think maybe I can.

1291
01:19:09,211 --> 01:19:10,702
- Uh, let's see, let's see.
- Great.

1292
01:19:10,879 --> 01:19:16,951
Here's... Here's a 20
and a 20 and two 5's

1293
01:19:17,319 --> 01:19:19,811
and a, uh, 50.

1294
01:19:19,989 --> 01:19:22,823
Oh, Mr. Matlock,
you're a lifesaver.

1295
01:19:23,959 --> 01:19:25,450
Thank you.

1296
01:19:31,433 --> 01:19:33,834
Is this the briefcase you gave
to the decedent that night?

1297
01:19:34,003 --> 01:19:35,096
Yes, it is.

1298
01:19:35,271 --> 01:19:36,796
What was in it when
you gave it to him?

1299
01:19:36,972 --> 01:19:39,373
One hundred thousand
dollars in $100 bills.

1300
01:19:40,342 --> 01:19:41,674
What's in it now?

1301
01:19:43,012 --> 01:19:46,039
- Nothing.
- Your Honor, we already know

1302
01:19:46,215 --> 01:19:49,777
that's the briefcase that was found
underneath the decedent's body.

1303
01:19:49,952 --> 01:19:51,818
It's already entered
into evidence.

1304
01:19:52,721 --> 01:19:54,189
Mr. Matlock?

1305
01:19:56,091 --> 01:19:59,687
Why didn't you come forward
when Ellen was arrested?

1306
01:20:03,599 --> 01:20:05,625
I was ashamed.

1307
01:20:06,735 --> 01:20:08,067
Nothing further.

1308
01:20:08,237 --> 01:20:10,331
No questions.

1309
01:20:10,673 --> 01:20:13,673
He told me a lot of things when he
and Ellen were going through the divorce.

1310
01:20:13,809 --> 01:20:17,075
He needed someone
to confide in, and I was it.

1311
01:20:17,546 --> 01:20:20,345
Uh, how do you make
a living, Mr. Jefferies?

1312
01:20:20,516 --> 01:20:22,348
I own a half
dozen textile mills.

1313
01:20:22,518 --> 01:20:24,077
Been in my family for years.

1314
01:20:24,453 --> 01:20:27,048
- They union or nonunion?
- Nonunion.

1315
01:20:28,023 --> 01:20:31,551
Uh, what would happen
if they went union?

1316
01:20:32,294 --> 01:20:34,889
I'd have to shut them down.
I couldn't afford to run them.

1317
01:20:36,198 --> 01:20:37,393
I don't know.

1318
01:20:37,566 --> 01:20:39,865
I don't know, I think
you could afford it.

1319
01:20:40,035 --> 01:20:44,735
It's just that you and your
CEOs wouldn't be able to draw

1320
01:20:44,907 --> 01:20:47,638
such nice big
salaries, would you?

1321
01:20:47,810 --> 01:20:50,336
That's the real reason
you scream "shut down"

1322
01:20:50,512 --> 01:20:53,846
every time that five-letter word
that starts with a U comes up, isn't it?

1323
01:20:54,383 --> 01:20:57,410
I pay my workers well,
and I treat them fairly.

1324
01:20:57,586 --> 01:21:00,055
- And they'd be the first to say so.
- Well, that may be,

1325
01:21:00,222 --> 01:21:04,557
but there is a movement to
unionize mills like yours, isn't there?

1326
01:21:04,727 --> 01:21:09,825
Like in those editorials
in the <i>Atlanta Herald?</i>

1327
01:21:09,998 --> 01:21:13,696
- Are they pro- or anti-union?
- Pro.

1328
01:21:13,969 --> 01:21:16,598
They keep fanning the
flames, don't they? Heh, heh.

1329
01:21:16,772 --> 01:21:17,796
Does that bother you?

1330
01:21:18,440 --> 01:21:19,806
Of course it bothers me.

1331
01:21:20,175 --> 01:21:22,110
Who's editor in
chief of the <i>Herald?</i>

1332
01:21:23,345 --> 01:21:24,438
Brad Gelardi.

1333
01:21:24,613 --> 01:21:27,481
Well, he's a friend of yours.

1334
01:21:27,816 --> 01:21:28,875
Didn't you talk to him,

1335
01:21:29,051 --> 01:21:32,715
try to get him to stop
publishing that stuff pro-union?

1336
01:21:33,088 --> 01:21:35,785
- Of course I did, but...
- He kept fanning the flames.

1337
01:21:35,958 --> 01:21:38,792
He kept fanning the flames,
didn't he? Yeah, yeah.

1338
01:21:38,961 --> 01:21:45,526
Flames that, uh, could
ruin your business, maybe.

1339
01:21:45,701 --> 01:21:48,830
Certainly your lifestyle.

1340
01:21:49,805 --> 01:21:52,070
And so since arguing
with him wouldn't help,

1341
01:21:52,241 --> 01:21:56,337
you decided on
more drastic moves.

1342
01:21:56,512 --> 01:22:00,779
You figured out a way
to not only get him fired,

1343
01:22:00,949 --> 01:22:02,508
but to ruin his character

1344
01:22:02,684 --> 01:22:04,484
and discredit him and
everything he stood for,

1345
01:22:04,653 --> 01:22:06,645
- didn't you?
- Objection!

1346
01:22:07,156 --> 01:22:09,955
What in the world do
mills and unions have to do

1347
01:22:10,125 --> 01:22:13,527
with whether or not Ellen Barrett
committed first degree murder?

1348
01:22:13,695 --> 01:22:16,722
I just got started, Peter.
For heaven's sakes.

1349
01:22:16,899 --> 01:22:18,390
Mr. Matlock.

1350
01:22:18,567 --> 01:22:21,127
I'll tie it up, Your
Honor. I promise.

1351
01:22:21,770 --> 01:22:24,001
Proceed. Objection overruled.

1352
01:22:26,275 --> 01:22:29,677
I understand you like to
water ski, Mr. Jefferies.

1353
01:22:30,446 --> 01:22:31,470
Yes, I do.

1354
01:22:31,847 --> 01:22:35,306
The decedent, Adam
Easley, liked to water ski.

1355
01:22:35,484 --> 01:22:36,975
- Did you know that?
- No.

1356
01:22:37,152 --> 01:22:40,281
Oh, yeah. He had a ski boat down
at Lake George and everything.

1357
01:22:40,456 --> 01:22:41,480
You got a ski boat?

1358
01:22:42,291 --> 01:22:44,817
- Yes, I do.
- Oh.

1359
01:22:44,993 --> 01:22:47,473
He got a ski boat, you got a ski
boat. Where do you keep yours?

1360
01:22:47,629 --> 01:22:48,688
Lake George.

1361
01:22:48,864 --> 01:22:50,389
Did you know Adam Easley?

1362
01:22:50,566 --> 01:22:51,898
No.

1363
01:22:52,634 --> 01:22:54,068
Well, there's a man here

1364
01:22:54,236 --> 01:22:57,138
who docked his boat
next to Adam Easley's boat,

1365
01:22:57,306 --> 01:22:58,604
and he's prepared to testify

1366
01:22:58,774 --> 01:23:01,039
that he saw you and
Mr. Easley together

1367
01:23:01,210 --> 01:23:02,508
on numerous occasions.

1368
01:23:02,678 --> 01:23:04,772
- He's mistaken.
- Objection.

1369
01:23:04,947 --> 01:23:09,544
We got mills and unions
and water skiing and boats.

1370
01:23:09,718 --> 01:23:12,347
Just when does Mr. Matlock
plan to get around to something

1371
01:23:12,521 --> 01:23:13,721
that is relevant to this case?

1372
01:23:13,889 --> 01:23:16,654
Mr. Haskins, give me a break.

1373
01:23:16,825 --> 01:23:18,157
You did say something

1374
01:23:18,327 --> 01:23:21,263
about tying all this up,
didn't you, Mr. Matlock?

1375
01:23:23,031 --> 01:23:24,522
Yes, sir.

1376
01:23:25,167 --> 01:23:28,899
Were you aware that Ellen
Barrett plagiarized the articles

1377
01:23:29,071 --> 01:23:31,836
- that won her the Pulitzer Prize?
- No.

1378
01:23:33,008 --> 01:23:34,601
Well, that's odd.

1379
01:23:34,776 --> 01:23:37,837
I understood that your
friend, Brad Gelardi,

1380
01:23:38,013 --> 01:23:40,915
told you they were
plagiarized and that he knew it.

1381
01:23:41,884 --> 01:23:43,352
Um...

1382
01:23:43,619 --> 01:23:45,520
Yeah, actually I
think he did tell me.

1383
01:23:45,687 --> 01:23:49,124
Actually, he's
prepared to testify

1384
01:23:49,358 --> 01:23:52,851
that in the middle of his divorce
when you were his closest friend,

1385
01:23:53,028 --> 01:23:54,724
- he told you.
- I guess I forgot.

1386
01:23:55,030 --> 01:23:58,831
Yeah. And you decided
to take that knowledge

1387
01:23:59,001 --> 01:24:00,970
that he trusted you with

1388
01:24:01,136 --> 01:24:04,106
and use it to nip this
union business in the bud

1389
01:24:04,273 --> 01:24:06,401
by framing him for murder

1390
01:24:07,142 --> 01:24:10,943
and by exposing him as a
man who condoned plagiarism.

1391
01:24:11,113 --> 01:24:13,639
- At least, that was the plan, wasn't it?
- No.

1392
01:24:13,815 --> 01:24:18,810
And so you obtained a
copy of the Chinese book

1393
01:24:18,987 --> 01:24:20,421
that Ellen had plagiarized

1394
01:24:20,589 --> 01:24:25,220
and gave it to your water-skiing
buddy, Adam Easley,

1395
01:24:25,394 --> 01:24:30,264
and had him make the call to Brad
about the blackmail money, didn't you?

1396
01:24:30,432 --> 01:24:31,923
- No.
- You told him to ask

1397
01:24:32,100 --> 01:24:35,662
for $100,000 in cash.

1398
01:24:36,038 --> 01:24:40,203
Then you arranged for Mr. Easley
to meet Brad at that motel

1399
01:24:40,375 --> 01:24:41,399
and collect it.

1400
01:24:41,577 --> 01:24:43,944
Not one word of this is true.

1401
01:24:44,112 --> 01:24:45,637
After Brad left,

1402
01:24:46,848 --> 01:24:50,410
you showed up, wearing gloves.

1403
01:24:50,886 --> 01:24:52,946
And you did something

1404
01:24:53,121 --> 01:24:56,785
that Adam Easley didn't
expect at all, didn't you?

1405
01:24:58,060 --> 01:24:59,619
You picked up the steak knife

1406
01:24:59,795 --> 01:25:01,764
- and killed him!
- No!

1407
01:25:01,930 --> 01:25:04,957
He was holding the briefcase
and, good for you, he fell on it.

1408
01:25:05,133 --> 01:25:06,999
I didn't kill him.
I wasn't there.

1409
01:25:07,736 --> 01:25:10,171
You removed the
money from the briefcase,

1410
01:25:10,339 --> 01:25:14,003
put the case back under the
body so the police would assume

1411
01:25:14,176 --> 01:25:15,337
that Brad was the killer.

1412
01:25:15,510 --> 01:25:16,534
And then you left.

1413
01:25:16,712 --> 01:25:18,146
That's not true.

1414
01:25:19,514 --> 01:25:21,676
What you didn't know

1415
01:25:22,184 --> 01:25:25,882
is that Brad had told
Ellen about the blackmail,

1416
01:25:26,054 --> 01:25:29,616
and she was afraid that
somehow he might botch the payoff.

1417
01:25:29,791 --> 01:25:34,126
So after you left the
motel, she showed up,

1418
01:25:34,863 --> 01:25:36,764
saw the body,

1419
01:25:36,932 --> 01:25:39,800
and she assumed what you
wanted everybody else to assume,

1420
01:25:39,968 --> 01:25:42,096
that Brad had done it.

1421
01:25:42,270 --> 01:25:45,638
So to protect Brad and herself,

1422
01:25:45,807 --> 01:25:50,871
she staged that scene to
make it look like self-defense.

1423
01:25:51,313 --> 01:25:54,408
When all the time,
it was you, wasn't it?

1424
01:25:54,583 --> 01:25:57,917
- No. This is outrageous.
- Objection! Proof!

1425
01:25:58,086 --> 01:26:01,284
Proof! It is obvious to me
Mr. Matlock has absolutely

1426
01:26:01,456 --> 01:26:04,187
no concept of the word "proof."

1427
01:26:06,028 --> 01:26:10,796
Mr. Haskins, I've been an officer
of the court for my entire adult life.

1428
01:26:10,966 --> 01:26:13,526
Do you really think I'd
go this far without proof?

1429
01:26:15,671 --> 01:26:16,695
How about it?

1430
01:26:17,839 --> 01:26:20,866
All right, right here.

1431
01:26:21,043 --> 01:26:23,945
Right here in this little sack.

1432
01:26:24,112 --> 01:26:27,674
What has $100 bill
got to do with anything?

1433
01:26:27,849 --> 01:26:31,513
He said, "What's $100
bill got to do with anything?"

1434
01:26:31,987 --> 01:26:33,979
I was just getting to that.

1435
01:26:34,156 --> 01:26:36,625
Scout's honor, Your Honor.

1436
01:26:38,326 --> 01:26:40,420
Go ahead, fast.

1437
01:26:40,595 --> 01:26:42,029
Okay.

1438
01:26:43,231 --> 01:26:45,723
You know where I got
this $100 bill, Mr. Jefferies?

1439
01:26:46,101 --> 01:26:47,763
I have no idea.

1440
01:26:47,936 --> 01:26:49,734
Your wife gave it to me.

1441
01:26:53,008 --> 01:26:56,843
Detective Marks saw her
give it to me the other day

1442
01:26:57,012 --> 01:27:00,210
in exchange for
some smaller bills.

1443
01:27:00,549 --> 01:27:02,848
It occurred to me

1444
01:27:03,018 --> 01:27:06,750
that it might be
one of $100 bills

1445
01:27:06,922 --> 01:27:10,757
that Brad took to Adam
Easley the night of the murder.

1446
01:27:10,926 --> 01:27:14,988
And I hoped the lab
could find fingerprints.

1447
01:27:15,163 --> 01:27:17,428
What they found was even better.

1448
01:27:17,599 --> 01:27:18,623
Know what it was?

1449
01:27:19,768 --> 01:27:21,634
Again, I have no idea.

1450
01:27:24,406 --> 01:27:27,774
Traces of Adam Easley's blood.

1451
01:27:28,744 --> 01:27:31,942
It was part of the
missing blackmail money.

1452
01:27:33,448 --> 01:27:35,747
How'd your wife end up with it?

1453
01:27:36,418 --> 01:27:37,511
You gave it to her.

1454
01:27:38,520 --> 01:27:40,648
How did you end up with it?

1455
01:27:40,822 --> 01:27:44,953
It was part of the money you took
out of that blood-soaked briefcase

1456
01:27:45,127 --> 01:27:48,393
after you stabbed
Adam Easley to death.

1457
01:27:49,164 --> 01:27:51,599
Wasn't it, Mr. Jefferies?

1458
01:27:54,836 --> 01:27:56,862
No more questions.

1459
01:28:08,450 --> 01:28:12,444
We find the defendant,
Ellen Barrett, not guilty.

1460
01:28:13,088 --> 01:28:14,368
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

1461
01:28:14,523 --> 01:28:18,153
Bail is exonerated and
the defendant is free to go.

1462
01:28:19,928 --> 01:28:21,487
Mom.

1463
01:28:27,435 --> 01:28:28,459
Congratulations.

1464
01:28:28,637 --> 01:28:30,265
Thank you.

1465
01:28:35,777 --> 01:28:39,270
Okay, I'm taking
both of you to lunch.

1466
01:28:39,447 --> 01:28:42,975
Now that you're both unemployed
and we have nothing better to do,

1467
01:28:43,151 --> 01:28:46,747
maybe we can finally learn
how to talk to one another.

1468
01:28:59,568 --> 01:29:01,264
Go to lunch?

1469
01:29:01,770 --> 01:29:03,864
After whipping
my tail like that?

1470
01:29:04,039 --> 01:29:05,598
Making a fool out of me?

1471
01:29:05,774 --> 01:29:08,300
Humiliating me in
front of my peers?

1472
01:29:08,476 --> 01:29:11,105
- I'm buying.
- I'm easy.

1473
01:29:33,468 --> 01:29:35,699
You think we'll
all fit in that car?

1474
01:29:35,871 --> 01:29:37,430
It'll be a tight squeeze.

1475
01:29:37,606 --> 01:29:40,440
No worse than the cheap
seats on most airplanes.

1476
01:29:40,609 --> 01:29:43,443
Lord knows you're used to them.

1477
01:29:44,512 --> 01:29:46,174
Oh, I don't know, Ben.

1478
01:29:46,348 --> 01:29:47,782
What?

1479
01:29:48,516 --> 01:29:52,954
Think I should have sold
Lucy's house like that?

1480
01:29:54,055 --> 01:29:58,254
It's a little late for
seller's remorse, Billy.

1481
01:29:58,426 --> 01:30:02,830
Yeah, but she was my sister.

1482
01:30:03,331 --> 01:30:06,392
She spent her last years here.

1483
01:30:06,801 --> 01:30:09,066
Now, thanks to me,

1484
01:30:09,237 --> 01:30:12,173
they're gonna tear it all
down and plow it under

1485
01:30:12,340 --> 01:30:15,640
like some big patch of weeds.

1486
01:30:17,279 --> 01:30:19,180
Maybe I shouldn't have sold it.

1487
01:30:19,347 --> 01:30:22,476
Maybe I should've
just let it sit.

1488
01:30:23,218 --> 01:30:26,985
- Kind of like the Lincoln Memorial.
- Yeah.

1489
01:30:29,024 --> 01:30:33,724
Billy, I think it's good for
you to feel guilty like this.

1490
01:30:33,895 --> 01:30:37,332
And I don't wish to
speak evil of the dead,

1491
01:30:38,066 --> 01:30:41,036
but I think you're
forgetting something here.

1492
01:30:41,603 --> 01:30:44,869
Lucy was nasty,

1493
01:30:45,740 --> 01:30:50,337
mean, ornery,
hateful, muleheaded.

1494
01:30:50,845 --> 01:30:54,612
And you had to live
with her for 40 years.

1495
01:30:54,783 --> 01:30:59,153
You gave up 40 years
of your life. Forty years.

1496
01:31:00,488 --> 01:31:02,650
You hated her, Billy.

1497
01:31:04,826 --> 01:31:06,624
Thanks, Ben.

1498
01:31:07,062 --> 01:31:08,860
I needed that.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
